{"type":"tool_result","callId":"chatcmpl-tool-934443f7478e0ed1","status":"completed","result":"<path>/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Skilled/.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/SKILL.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: name: deep-review\n3: description: \"Autonomous iterative code-review loop with externalized state, convergence detection, P0/P1/P2 findings, fresh context per pass.\"\n4: argument-hint: \"[target] [:auto|:confirm] [--max-iterations=N] [--convergence=N] [--stop-policy=convergence|max-iterations]\"\n5: version: 1.11.14.0\n6: allowed-tools: [Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob, Task]\n7: ---\n8: <!-- Note: Task is for the command executor (loop management); @deep-review agent is LEAF-only (no Task). No WebFetch: review is code-only. -->\n9: \n10: # Autonomous Deep Review Loop\n11: \n12: Iterative code review and quality auditing protocol with fresh context per iteration, externalized state, convergence detection, and severity-weighted findings (P0/P1/P2).\n13: \n14: Runtime path resolution:\n15: - OpenCode/Copilot runtime: `.opencode/agents/*.md`\n16: - Claude runtime: `.claude/agents/*.md`\n17: \n18: Convergence threshold semantics and sibling-parity notes (deep-review 0.10 vs deep-research 0.05 vs deep-ai-council 0.20) live in `references/convergence/convergence.md` §1 under \"Threshold Semantics and Sibling Parity\".\n19: \n20: ## 1. WHEN TO USE\n21: \n22: ### When to Use This Skill\n23: \n24: Use this skill when:\n25: - Code quality audit requiring multiple rounds across different review dimensions\n26: - Spec folder validation requiring cross-reference checks between docs and implementation\n27: - Release readiness check before shipping a feature or component\n28: - Finding misalignments between spec documents and actual code\n29: - Verifying cross-references across documentation, agents, commands, and code\n30: - Iterative review where each dimension's findings inform subsequent dimensions\n31: - Unattended or overnight audit sessions\n32: \n33: ### When NOT to Use\n34: \n35: - Simple single-pass code review (use `sk-code`'s code-review mode instead)\n36: - Known issues that just need fixing (go directly to implementation)\n37: - Implementation tasks (use `sk-code` or `/speckit:implement`)\n38: - Quick one-file checks (use direct Grep/Read)\n39: - Fewer than 2 review dimensions needed (single-pass suffices)\n40: \n41: ### FORBIDDEN INVOCATION PATTERNS\n42: \n43: This skill is invoked EXCLUSIVELY through the `/deep:review` command. The command's YAML workflow owns state, dispatch, and convergence.\n44: \n45: **NEVER:**\n46: - Write a custom bash/shell dispatcher to parallelize iterations (ad-hoc shell fan-out)\n47: - Invoke any CLI executor (cli-codex, cli-claude-code, cli-opencode, cli-cursor, cli-devin, cli-pi, cli-deepseek-harness) directly in a loop to simulate iterations\n48: - Manually write iteration prompts to `/tmp` and dispatch them via `copilot -p`\n49: - Dispatch the `@deep-review` LEAF agent via the Task tool for iteration loops (the agent is LEAF, a single iteration, and MUST be driven by the command's workflow)\n50: - Skip the state machine: `deep-review-state.jsonl`, `deep-review-config.json`, `deltas/`, `prompts/`, `logs/`\n51: - Manage iteration state outside the resolved local review packet under `{spec_folder}/review/`\n52: \n53: **COMMAND-DRIVEN FAN-OUT IS SUPPORTED:** use `--executor`/`--executors`/`--concurrency` flags on `/deep:review`. The command's YAML `step_fanout_spawn` owns multi-lineage dispatch; `fanout-merge.cjs` applies strongest-restriction (any lineage active P0 → merged FAIL). This is not ad-hoc shell dispatch — it is the canonical fan-out path. Intra-lineage wave orchestration remains deferred.\n54: \n55: **ALWAYS:**\n56: - Invoke via `/deep:review :auto` or `/deep:review :confirm`\n57: - Let the command's YAML workflow own dispatch (auto: `.skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-review-auto.yaml`)\n58: - Record each leaf iteration through the append gateway (`runtime/scripts/append-mode-event.cjs --mode review --run-directory <spec folder> --event-json <file>`) — the gateway is the SINGLE state-log writer: it authorizes, fences, and receipts the write, then refreshes `{state_paths.state_log}` from the ledger\n59: - Treat `scripts/reduce-state.cjs` as the reducer for DERIVED artifacts only (finding registry, dashboard metrics, strategy updates); it never writes the state log\n60: - Require every iteration to produce BOTH the markdown narrative AND the JSONL delta (dispatch scripts must fail if either is missing)\n61: - Use `resolveArtifactRoot(specFolder, 'review')` from `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/shared/review-research-paths.cjs` to locate the canonical review root\n62: \n63: ### Trigger Phrases\n64: \n65: - \"review code quality\" / \"audit this code\"\n66: - \"audit spec folder\" / \"validate spec completeness\"\n67: - \"release readiness check\" / \"pre-release review\"\n68: - \"find misalignments\" (between spec and implementation)\n69: - \"verify cross-references\" (across docs and code)\n70: - \"deep review\" / \"iterative review\" / \"review loop\"\n71: - \"quality audit\" / \"convergence detection\"\n72: \n73: ### Keyword Triggers\n74: \n75: `deep review`, `convergence review`, `iterative review`, `review loop`, `release readiness`, `spec folder review`, `convergence detection`, `quality audit`, `find misalignments`, `verify cross-references`, `pre-release review`, `audit spec folder`\n76: \n77: ---\n78: \n79: ## 2. SMART ROUTING\n80: \n81: The `system-deep-loop` hub routes an iterative review loop here, and its `review` mode signal selects this packet.\n82: The signal fires when a request names a deep review, a convergence review, a review loop, a release readiness check or a spec-folder audit.\n83: The loop runs only through `/deep:review`, whose workflow owns state, dispatch and convergence.\n84: \n85: `references/protocol/quick-reference.md` loads first on every invocation.\n86: The review phase named by the dispatch context then selects the loop protocol, the convergence documents or the state documents.\n87: \n88: The routing pseudocode in [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) is the one route from a request to a resource set.\n89: A request whose intent scores stay under the threshold returns `UNKNOWN_FALLBACK` with a disambiguation checklist, never a silent default.\n90: Read [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting skill-local resources; open only the matched files.\n91: \n92: ---\n93: \n94: ## 3. HOW IT WORKS\n95: \n96: ### Architecture\n97: \n98: `/deep:review` owns the loop. The YAML workflow initializes state, dispatches one LEAF review iteration at a time, evaluates convergence, synthesizes `review-report.md`, and saves continuity. The LEAF agent reads state, reviews one dimension, writes `iteration-NNN.md`, updates strategy, and appends JSONL. Full 4-phase lifecycle: `references/protocol/loop-protocol.md`.\n99: \n100: ### Resource Map Coverage Gate\n101: \n102: When `{spec_folder}/resource-map.md` exists at init, deep review treats it as a mandatory audit input and does four things:\n103: \n104: - Persists `resource_map_present` in config.\n105: - Seeds `Known Context` with a snapshot of the map.\n106: - Has at least one iteration audit `target_files` coverage against the map: touched, not-touched with `expected-by-scope`-versus-`gap`, and absent-from-map, with findings tagged `resource-map-coverage`.\n107: - Has synthesis add the `## Resource Map Coverage Gate` report section and a converged `resource-map.md`. Disable this with `--no-resource-map`.\n108: \n109: Absent at init, the gate and section are skipped without failing the loop.\n110: Full sequencing: `references/protocol/loop-protocol.md` (init 9a, iteration 3b, synthesis 4). Schema: `references/state/state-format.md`.\n111: \n112: ### Bounded Context Snapshot Replacement\n113: \n114: During initialization, capture a bounded, pointer-based context snapshot in `deep-review-strategy.md` `Known Context` before the first dimension runs.\n115: The snapshot holds:\n116: \n117: - Target pointers: files, specs, symbols and resource-map entries.\n118: - The claimed behavior and acceptance criteria to verify.\n119: - Reuse and convention pointers.\n120: - Risk areas, missing context, stale-graph caveats and out-of-scope areas.\n121: \n122: Do not create a context-loop report or widen scope beyond the declared target.\n123:","truncated":true}
