{"type":"thinking","text":"The manifest lists 74 files in scope. My review focus is documentation alignment against skills. Note some files in manifest are documentation/skills, and others are code.\n\nWait — the manifest does NOT include skill docs like `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill/...` or `.skilled/skills/sk-code/...`. Hmm, the review focus says \"Scope is exactly goal-file-manifest.txt in the spec folder. Check alignment: code against .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled; SKILL.md, references, feature-catalog and manual-testing-playbook against .skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-skill, sk-create-feature-catalog and sk-create-manual-testing-playbook; command docs and presentation assets against sk-doc/sk-create-command; READMEs including root README.md against sk-doc/sk-create-readme.\"\n\nSo the scope is the manifest files (which includes `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/...` docs and command assets and root README/AGENTS), and we check alignment against the *standards skills* (sk-doc/sk-create-* etc.), which are read-only references. Those standards skills are not in the manifest, but are the yardstick. Good.\n\nHighest yield: docs stale against phases 006-010 behavior, which SKILL.md, references, changelog, feature catalog, playbook and README were never updated for: relocate.mjs exit 3, late-step ledger marks and resume, case-folded duplicate refusal, phase-map bare-name and re-parent rewrites, pre-archive id rewrite, mixed-track restore refusal, archive-aware phase numbering, archivedPaths in path JSON, suggest-subtracks refusing non NNN-slug packets, reworded archive signal. Cite exact file:line for each gap; also say which docs need no change.\n\nI need to review the manifest files, especially system-spec-kit docs and code, against phases 006-010. This is a big documentation alignment review. I have 5 iterations. Let me plan:\n\n- Iteration 1: Inventory + correctness of code surfaces in manifest vs goal/spec phases 006-010 (relocate.mjs, create.sh, suggest-subtracks.mjs, rg wrapper, validate.sh, archive). Focus correctness, and the operator focus (docs alignment) mapping.\n- Iteration 2: Doc alignment traceability: SKILL.md, references (folder-structure, phase-definitions, quick-reference, worked-examples, retrieval-conventions), changelog, rack children lists.\n- Iteration 3: Feature catalog + manual testing playbook alignment.\n- Iteration 4: Command docs + presentation assets + READMEs (system-spec-kit README, root README, AGENTS.md) against sk-create-* standards.\n- Iteration 5: Security/maintainability + cross-reference consolidation, verdict, remaining findings.\n\nBut the dimensions configured are correctness, security, traceability, maintainability. Each iteration covers one dimension plus the review focus. I can interleave: Iter 1 correctness (code behavior vs phase goals), Iter 2 traceability (docs stale vs behavior), Iter 3 maintainability (feature catalog/playbook/README structure against sk-doc standards), Iter 4 security (archive search path handling, rg flags, restore refusals) + docs, Iter 5 cross-reference/synthesis.\n\nActually the workflow's convergence: with 5 iterations max, 4 dimensions cover in 4 iterations, then iteration 5 is extra/cross-reference. Given the docs staleness is the highest yield, I should ensure enough budget for reading many docs.\n\nI need to be efficient: each iteration has a tool call budget of 9-13 calls. But as the orchestrator in a single session, I also need to write files, run gateway and reducer per iteration. The gateway + reducer are bash calls. Budget-wise, iteration calls are effectively unlimited for me as the orchestrator, but the persona says max 13 tool calls per iteration. Hmm, I'm executing the iteration inline as the executor. The lineage is more important. I'll aim for focused review: ~8-12 read/grep calls per iteration plus writes/admin. I can batch reads in one bash call? The instructions say use read tool for text files, not shell cat. But I can read multiple files via multiple read calls in one message (parallel). That counts as multiple calls but they happen in one turn. I'll try to keep it reasonable: per iteration maybe 10-14 tool calls total including writes. Since I'm the orchestrator and executor, I need to satisfy the artifact contract more than the tool-call budget. The workflow says \"Target 9 tool calls. Soft max 12, hard max 13\" — per iteration. I'll count review actions, and admin (gateway/reducer) is separate workflow machinery. I think it's acceptable to exceed slightly for the orchestrator; but better to batch.\n\nLet me now read the key files. First, the target spec's spec.md, goal.md, and phase goals 006-010 to know what behavior changed; then the code files relocate.mjs, create.sh, suggest-subtracks.mjs, archive.sh; then docs. This is a lot. Let me be systematic.\n\nAlso need to look at the review-core doctrine. Let me read that (needed before severity calls). And the config template + strategy template.\n\nLet me first check .executor-state and containment, locks — maybe fanout-run created some metadata. Also check if steer.md appears later. Then start init.\n\nPlan for init:\n1. mkdir iterations, deltas, prompts (prompts optional but template path; I'll create).\n2. Write deep-review-config.json in lineage dir. Copy template populated with fields: mode review, sessionId fanout-dsh-2-1794652127582-mfqldk, parentSessionId null, lineageMode new, generation 1, continuedFromRun null, reviewTarget, reviewTargetType spec-folder, reviewDimensions all four, reviewFocus the focus text, resource_map_present false (check target spec folder for resource-map.md — not in ls, so false), reviewScopeFiles = manifest paths, severityThreshold P2, crossReference core/overlay, maxIterations 5, convergenceThreshold 0.1, antiConvergence mode default, stopPolicy convergence, stuckThreshold 2, maxDurationMinutes 120, specFolder, resource_map.emit true, createdAt now, status initialized, releaseReadinessState in-progress, executionMode auto, and importantly fanout: config.fanout? The lineage config should probably include `fanout` so step_fanout_merge/emit_resource_map skip at synthesis. But I'm running synthesis inside the lineage; step_fanout_merge is skipped for lineage execution (the base workflow handles merge). The instruction says run phase_init, phase_main_loop, phase_synthesis. The lineage's config template from fanout-run presumably includes fanout marker. Let me check fanout-run.cjs to see what config it writes for each lineage (or does it pass config.fanout_lineage_artifact_dir and the child process runs the same workflow?). Actually for CLI lineages, fanout-run spawns the CLI with a prompt similar to mine. The lineage prompt says \"run phase_init, phase_main_loop, phase_synthesis\" and \"The step_resolve_artifact_root step will bind artifact_dir to override — do NOT run the resolveArtifactRoot node command\". The config.fanout in the lineage is probably absent, since merge is at root. For step_emit_resource_map, skip_when \"config.fanout is present\". Hmm, if fanout absent in lineage config, it would run reducer with --emit-resource-map into lineage's resource-map.md — that's fine and inside lineage. But step_fanout_merge skip_when config.fanout absent would try to merge base artifact dir... Actually step_fanout_merge is the workflow's synthesis step for the root. In the lineage, we skip it as it's the root's job. The prompt says run phase_synthesis, which in a lineage context means compile the lineage report. The base runner earlier says \"After all lineages complete, jump directly to phase_synthesis — skip phase_init and phase_main_loop entirely... The merge step in phase_synthesis will consolidate results across all lineages.\" That's the root. As a lineage, I should do my own synthesis without step_fanout_merge. I'll skip fanout merge, emit resource map (safe, inside lineage), and compile report.\n\nActually, wait. Will fanout-merge later expect each lineage's findings registry and state log; yes, it reads lineages/*/deep-review-findings-registry.json. The lineage synthesis writes review-report.md and dashboard. Fine.\n\n3. Open run through gateway: need a config row first? The workflow step_create","truncated":true}
