{"type":"thinking","text":"Important: `parseIterationMarkdownFindings` looks for a section `## Findings` and subheadings `### N. ...` or numbered lines or F### bullets. The prompt pack says structure: \"headings for Dimension, Files Reviewed, Findings by Severity (P0/P1/P2), Traceability Checks, Verdict, Next Dimension.\" The agent contract says `### P0 Findings`, `### P1 Findings`, `### P2 Findings` with numbered bullets. So my iteration files must include `## Findings` heading and `### P0 Findings` etc. But the parser looks for `### N. Title` — which is a numbered subheading, not `### P0 Findings`. Hmm, different formats. The parser has fallbacks: numbered lines under `## Findings` at left margin (e.g., \"1. **Title** -- file:line -- ...\"). That matches the agent contract's numbered bullets. So I'll use `## Findings` then `### P0 Findings` and numbered lines `1. **Title** — file:line — desc`. Wait, but numbered subheadings regex `^###\\s+\\d+\\.\\s+(.+)$` would not match \"### P0 Findings\". Numbered lines regex `^\\d+\\.\\s+(.+)$` matches \"1. **Title** ...\". So the first shape that has any findings is numberedFindings. Good: if I use numbered lines under `### P0 Findings`, the parser collects all numbered lines across the Findings section. That works.\n\nAlso the claim adjudication step: every new P0/P1 needs typed packets. I need to include those JSON blocks in iteration files.\n\nThe reducer: I should check how it reads delta files and what it needs. Let me look at the main reducer CLI section and how it handles missing artifacts, plus strategy anchors. Let me read around line 2400-2500 of reduce-state.cjs and the review reduction function's delta parsing."}
