{"type":"thinking","text":"Now I have a good picture. The key documentation files that likely need updates: SKILL.md, references (folder-structure, phase-definitions, quick-reference, worked-examples, retrieval-conventions), feature catalog pages (spec-packet-relocation, sub-track-proposal, spec-lifecycle-automation, track-root-children-lists, rg-archive-search, spec-validation-rule-engine, feature-catalog.md), manual testing playbook pages (same), changelog v2.13.0.0, spec README, system-spec-kit README, root README, AGENTS.md, command docs (search.md, speckit README, presentations), ENV-REFERENCE, retrieval conventions.\n\nThe review focus: \"Highest yield: docs stale against phases 006-010 behaviour, which SKILL.md, references, changelog, feature catalog, playbook and README were never updated for: relocate.mjs exit 3, late-step ledger marks and resume, case-folded duplicate refusal, phase-map bare-name and re-parent rewrites, pre-archive id rewrite, mixed-track restore refusal, archive-aware phase numbering, archivedPaths in path JSON, suggest-subtracks refusing non NNN-slug packets, reworded archive signal. Cite exact file:line for each gap; also say which docs need no change.\"\n\nSo the review is primarily a documentation-accuracy review. This is a traceability/maintainability review mostly. P1 findings for stale docs that misstate behavior? Given the completion criteria for the project, validate.sh --strict --recursive passes — docs stale but consistent. Severity: stale feature docs that claim behavior contradicted by code is likely P1 (degraded behavior / incorrect documentation) or P2 (documentation gaps). The review-core reach calibration: P1 for incorrect docs that mislead operators? In this review, docs are the product surface for skills; a doc that says `relocate` exits 2 on failure when it now exits 3 for validation-only failure is a documentation correctness issue. Hmm. The user's review focus emphasizes docs stale — likely they expect P1 findings for gaps where docs were never updated (feature catalog/playbook are supposed to be auditable, with source anchors). Let me look at what severity is appropriate per review-core. I'll read review-core.md.\n\nAlso need to check the actual code behavior (the claimed new behavior) and compare docs. Let me gather the evidence systematically.\n\nFirst read review-core.md (doctrine). Then start init.\n\nActually, let me init first so the state exists, then do iterations. The init requires:\n- config file\n- gateway run open\n- findings registry\n- strategy\n- dirs\n\nI have not read the reference's quick-reference etc. It's fine. Let me read review-core now, and then write config. Also check resource-map.md presence: target folder has no resource-map.md (from ls: no). So resource_map_present false.\n\nFor review config, I'll model on the template plus fields. I need `reviewScopeFiles` maybe not in template but state-format mentions. The workflow populate includes reviewScopeFiles. I'll include it.\n\nSession id: fanout-dsh-2-1794652127582-mfqldk. Run id: the synthesis closeout uses basename(dirname(stateLog)) as scopeRunId, so run id is \"dsh-2\" when stateLog is .../lineages/dsh-2/deep-review-state.jsonl. That's okay.\n\nThe config row projection: the gateway will project a thin config row with type config? Let's test the run_initialized event. First create config. Then run the gateway with the run-open event file (mirroring step_create_state_log). Let me do that.\n\nBefore that, read review-core for severity."}
{"type":"tool_call","callId":"chatcmpl-tool-b87d1e2c31997784","tool":"read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Skilled/.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-review/references/review-core.md"}}
{"type":"tool_result","callId":"chatcmpl-tool-b87d1e2c31997784","status":"completed","result":"<path>/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Skilled/.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-review/references/review-core.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: title: Review Core Doctrine\n3: description: Shared findings-first review doctrine for both single-pass and deep-review workflows.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"findings first severity ordering\"\n6:   - \"review finding schema\"\n7:   - \"severity contract for merge decisions\"\n8:   - \"evidence file line citation\"\n9:   - \"baseline surface precedence\"\n10:   - \"baseline check families\"\n11: importance_tier: important\n12: contextType: implementation\n13: version: 1.5.9.0\n14: ---\n15: \n16: # Review Core Doctrine\n17: \n18: Shared doctrine consumed by both `@review` and `@deep-review`.\n19: \n20: **Ownership.** This file owns the severity ids and their meanings (section 2), the evidence rules (section 3) and the finding schema (section 7). The deep-review contract, [`review-mode-contract.yaml`](../../../system-deep-loop/deep-review/assets/review-mode-contract.yaml), is an external consumer: it owns the deep-review loop (dimensions, verdicts, severity weights, convergence and quality gates) and reuses the severity ids defined here. When the two disagree on a severity id or its meaning, this file wins and the YAML is corrected by its owner, `deep-review`. The YAML may be stricter for its own loop, as when it requires `file:line` evidence on `P2`, but never looser.\n21: \n22: ---\n23: \n24: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n25: \n26: ### Purpose\n27: \n28: Shared findings-first review doctrine for both single-pass and deep-review workflows.\n29: \n30: ### When to Use\n31: \n32: - A `@review` or `@deep-review` pass that needs the severity contract, the evidence rules or the finding schema.\n33: - A review that must state its `Rule sources:` line, or apply a repository or surface override to a default-lens rule.\n34: \n35: Every rule in sections 2 to 7 is review floor, the part no repository or surface can change. Section 8 names the floor ids, the default lens that a repository may drop or replace, and how the review reads and reports those overrides.\n36: \n37: ---\n38: \n39: ## 2. SEVERITY DEFINITIONS\n40: \n41: | Level | Meaning | Handling |\n42: | --- | --- | --- |\n43: | P0 | Blocker: exploitable security issue, auth bypass, destructive data loss | Block merge |\n44: | P1 | Required: correctness bug, spec mismatch, must-fix gate issue | Fix before merge |\n45: | P2 | Suggestion: non-blocking improvement, documentation polish, style or maintainability follow-up | Optional or schedule follow-up |\n46: \n47: ### Reach Calibration\n48: \n49: A `P0` or `P1` finding names its reach: an input or call path that the code's stated contract allows, or a real caller that sends the failing input. The stated contract is what the code promises through its signature, types, doc comment, name and the checks it already makes.\n50: \n51: - A failure that needs input outside the stated contract, such as `NaN` where the contract says a number, a wrong type, or an inverted range the code already rejects, is `P2` hardening.\n52: - The exception is input the code claims to validate. When a guard, a thrown error or a doc line promises to reject an input class and the promise fails for an input in that class, the gap is a correctness finding and keeps `P1` or higher. A guard claims only the class it checks: an ordering check such as `min > max` does not claim that its operands are numbers.\n53: - At a trust boundary (user input, network, files, environment, another service), every value the outside can send is in reach. The stated contract of internal code never narrows a security finding on data that crosses that boundary.\n54: - A finding that cannot name its reach is `P2`, however severe the failure would be.\n55: \n56: Escalation rule: if confidence is low but impact is high, classify toward the higher severity and state the uncertainty explicitly. The confidence is confidence that the reach exists. The rule raises a finding whose reach is named but not proven, such as a caller that probably sends the input. It never raises a finding with no reach, so it cannot lift an out-of-contract failure above `P2`.\n57: \n58: ### Numeric Severity Calibration\n59: \n60: Numeric scores are advisory context, not the gate. A reviewer may add an optional `riskScore` to a finding and adjust it by `+/-2` for local context such as exploitability, blast radius, user impact, confidence, or proven containment. The merge/block decision remains the severity contract above: `P0` blocks, `P1` requires remediation, and `P2` is advisory. Do not adopt `score>=4` or any numeric threshold as a blocker.\n61: \n62: ---\n63: \n64: ## 3. EVIDENCE REQUIREMENTS\n65: \n66: - Every `P0` and `P1` finding must include a concrete `file:line` citation.\n67: - Evidence must tie the finding to observed code behavior, not just a general concern.\n68: - `P2` findings should still include specific evidence when available, even if impact is advisory.\n69: - If evidence is incomplete, state the assumption and why the risk still matters.\n70: \n71: ---\n72: \n73: ## 4. FINDINGS OUTPUT ORDERING\n74: \n75: - Present findings before summary or praise sections.\n76: - Order findings by severity first: `P0`, then `P1`, then `P2`.\n77: - Keep ordering stable within a severity bucket by impact and confidence.\n78: - Separate required fixes from optional suggestions so merge decisions stay clear.\n79: \n80: ---\n81: \n82: ## 5. BASELINE + SURFACE PRECEDENCE\n83: \n84: Apply this skill as the baseline first, then pair it with `sk-code` surface evidence when available:\n85: \n86: - Detected code surfaces -> the surfaces the quality runner's resolver places each reviewed file in, specific surfaces first and the `SKILLED` fallback last. Each one pairs with its `sk-code` evidence packet, such as `sk-code-skilled` for `SKILLED`. No match -> `UNKNOWN`. The resolver applies the shared detection contract, [`stack-detection.md`](../../shared/references/stack-detection.md) section 2, so the review keeps no detection of its own. Section 8 describes what it returns.\n87: - Unclear surfaces -> baseline-only plus explicit uncertainty\n88: \n89: Precedence rules:\n90: \n91: - Baseline security and correctness minimums are always enforced.\n92: - Surface style, process, build, and test conventions override generic baseline guidance.\n93: - A repository or surface changes one default-lens rule only through a `## Rule overrides` row, by the precedence in section 8.\n94: - Unclear conflicts must be escalated rather than guessed.\n95: \n96: ---\n97: \n98: ## 6. BASELINE CHECK FAMILIES\n99: \n100: Mandatory baseline families:\n101: \n102: - Correctness minimums: regression risk, contract safety, spec mismatch, destructive side effects, and boundary handling.\n103: - Security minimums: auth and authorization gaps, injection exposure, unsafe secrets handling, privilege misuse, and reliability risks with security impact.\n104: \n105: These minimums cannot be relaxed by surface guidance.\n106: \n107: ---\n108: \n109: ## 7. FINDING SCHEMA\n110: \n111: Each finding should provide:\n112: \n113: | Field | Requirement |\n114: | --- | --- |\n115: | `id` | The finding's number in the Findings list, counted once across severity groups, such as `2` |\n116: | `severity` | One of `P0`, `P1`, `P2` |\n117: | `title` | Short, risk-oriented summary |\n118: | `case` | The input or situation that produces the wrong result. A finding with no case is not reported |\n119: | `file` | Primary `path:line` location |\n120: | `evidence` | Plain-language explanation tied to observed code; for a performance finding, name the workload it assumes, such as row count, request rate or payload size |\n121: | `userImpact` | What users or callers bear if this fix is deferred |\n122: | `findingClass` | One of `instance-only`, `class-of-bug`, `cross-consumer`, `algorithmic`, `matrix/evidence`, or `test-isolation` |\n123: | `scopeProof` | Grep/test/audit evidence that the recommendation covers same-class sites and consumers, or proves the finding is instance-only |\n124: | `affectedSurfaceHints` | Optional string array of producer/consumer surfaces the fix should address; recommended for actionable findings, required for cross-consumer findings. Use free-","truncated":true}
