{"type":"tool_result","callId":"chatcmpl-tool-8a9eab5a48c718c5","status":"completed","result":"8:  - \"hybrid search\"\n113:bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/create.sh 042-my-feature\n150:/speckit:search \"how did we decide on the auth architecture?\"\n153:The system reads the question, classifies the task intent and routes to the right search strategy automatically.\n185:| New feature, small scope | `create.sh NNN-name` | under 100 LOC, single file |\n186:| New feature, needs QA | `create.sh NNN-name` at Level 2 | 100 to 499 LOC |\n187:| Architecture change | `create.sh NNN-name` at Level 3 | 500+ LOC, multiple systems |\n188:| Multi-phase work | `create.sh NNN-name --phase` | large features, multiple sessions |\n191:| Check prior decisions | `/speckit:search \"query\"` | starting a related task |\n207:       ├─► Option B: Create new folder (create.sh)\n243:The track folder itself, `specs/<track>/`, holds no spec docs. Its `graph-metadata.json` lists the track's packets in `children_ids`. `create.sh --track` adds each new packet to that list, `archive.sh` refreshes it after moving a packet into the track's own `z_archive/` or back, `refresh-track-roots.mjs --apply` rewrites it from the packet folders on disk, and the pre-push hook blocks a pushed commit whose list disagrees with the packets it holds.\n245:A track can also hold one declared sub-track level, `specs/<track>/<sub-track>/<NNN-name>/`. A sub-track is a non-numbered child with no `spec.md`, `description.json` at level `sub-track` and the same `graph-metadata.json` shape as a track root, and the track lists it in `children_ids`. It numbers its own packets from `001`, keeps its own `z_archive/`, and is skipped with one diagnostic when it is a symlink. Undeclared non-numbered folders are reported, never promoted. One level only.\n263:A packet can opt into a `goal.md` document with `create.sh --with-goal`, valid at every level. With `--phase` it writes a goal into each child only, and `--level phase-parent --with-goal` writes a parent goal. `/create:goal` from `sk-create-goal` authors, amends or retrofits any goal. It holds the durable objective and completion criteria a session works toward across turns, rendered from `templates/addons/goal.md.tmpl`.\n305:Use `create.sh --phase` to create a parent with its first child in one step. Run `validate.sh --recursive` to validate the parent and all children together. The validator's phase-parent branch skips Level-N expectations on the lean parent: `check-files.sh`, `check-level-match.sh`, `check-template-source.sh` and the native `ANCHORS_VALID` rule. Tolerant migration policy: legacy phase parents that retain heavy docs continue to validate without churn.\n313:Templates live under `templates/` in three groups, `core/`, `addons/` and `packet-types/`, and render through the Level contract resolver. `create.sh` asks the resolver which files belong to each level, then the inline renderer expands only the sections allowed for that level.\n323:Optional support documents such as `handover.md`, `debug-delegation.md` and `research.md` render through the workflow that owns them. Templates use ANCHOR markers to mark logical sections. Validation checks required anchors, section ordering, template version alignment and cross-reference consistency. The `template-compliance-contract.md` reference defines which anchors are required at each level.\n325:`create.sh` rejects `--path` values that traverse outside the repository with a clear error before any write. Set `SPECKIT_POST_VALIDATE=1` when a strict workflow should run full validation immediately after scaffolding. A mkdir-based advisory lock protects `description.json` and `graph-metadata.json` writes during canonical save so two parallel `/speckit:save` calls for the same packet do not race.\n327:System Spec Kit has no memory MCP server. Its search pipeline, query intelligence, memory lifecycle, save scoring, causal graph, index self-maintenance and evaluation harness have no successor.\n332:- **Free-text retrieval:** the ripgrep recipes in `references/retrieval/retrieval-conventions.md` find a phrase anywhere in the corpus. The `rg-wrapper.mjs` structured recipe shows each file once at its best line, cuts at `--limit` files (default 20, `--limit 0` uncapped) and reports the hidden files and matches. The path-only and count recipes take the same limit. Archived packets are left out by default. `--include-archive` searches them as well, marks each archived path `[archived]` and lists the `description.json` and `graph-metadata.json` files of archived packets that hold the phrase.\n350:| `/speckit:search` | none | retrieval over spec docs using the ripgrep recipes, scoped by track and packet |\n351:| `/deep:research` | none | autonomous research loop with convergence detection |\n356:The tool counts `/speckit:save` and `/speckit:search` used to carry were counts of memory MCP tools. They are gone, so the column is `none` for both rather than a count.\n366:| `:with-research` | dispatch deep research before verification, `/speckit:complete` only |\n416:The engine resolves feature flags at call time rather than at import, so a long-lived process picks up an environment change without a restart. The flags that survive the retirement of the search store govern the shared embedding client and the runtime hook adapters:\n444:│   └── spec-kit-docs.json      # Level contract resolved by create.sh\n479:| [`runtime/cli/spec/create.sh`](./runtime/cli/spec/create.sh) | create spec folders with level-appropriate template files |\n526:`validate.sh` reports missing files such as `spec.md` or `plan.md`. This happens when the folder was created manually without `create.sh` or when wrong level templates were applied.\n540:  --glob '*.md' --glob '!**/z_archive/**' --glob '!**/node_modules/**' \\\n626:| `create.sh` | create spec folders with level-appropriate templates, `--phase` for parent plus child folders |\n627:| `validate.sh` | run the validation set from the 42-rule registry, `--strict` for strict-only rules, `--recursive` for phase folders, `--include-archive` with `--recursive` to also validate packets in the `z_archive/` of the folder given and, for a track root, of each declared sub-track, `--verbose` for details, `--quiet` for the result line only. A `--strict` run on a Draft packet whose only errors are derived metadata repairs it once and revalidates, so it can write files, and `SPECKIT_DRAFT_AUTO_REPAIR=0` turns that off |\n638:| `archive.sh` | archive completed spec folders into the `z_archive/` beside them, which for a packet in a declared sub-track is the sub-track's own `z_archive/`. `--restore` returns a folder to the place its archive belongs to, and when a live packet already holds its number it lands on the next free number, counting archived packets, through `relocate.mjs`. After an archive or a plain restore the description cache `specs/descriptions.json` is rebuilt when it exists |\n639:| `relocate.mjs` | move, renumber or re-parent spec packets and rewrite every store that records the path, from `--map <file.json>`, `--from` with `--to`, `--renumber <folder>` or `--declare-subtrack <track>/<name>`. Dry run by default, `--apply` writes |\n649:| `backfill-research-metadata.ts` | backfill missing metadata files under `research/*/iterations/` |\n695:| [`system-deep-loop`](../system-deep-loop/SKILL.md) | deep research and review loops that anchor into spec folders |\n703:| `.skilled/commands/speckit/` | speckit command definitions, including the continuity save and search commands |\n709:| [ripgrep](https://github.com/BurntSushi/ripgrep) | The free-text retrieval lane's search engine |\n"}
