{"type":"tool_result","callId":"chatcmpl-tool-b60119ebda538077","status":"completed","result":"<path>/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Skilled/.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/README.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Spec Scripts\"\n3: description: \"Spec lifecycle shell entrypoints for create, upgrade, validation, completion checks and archival.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"spec scripts\"\n6:   - \"upgrade spec level\"\n7:   - \"validate spec folder\"\n8:   - \"check placeholders\"\n9: ---\n10: \n11: # Spec Scripts\n12: \n13: ---\n14: \n15: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n16: \n17: `runtime/cli/spec/` owns shell entrypoints for spec folder lifecycle work. It creates packet folders, upgrades documentation levels, validates structure, checks completion state and archives finished or stale folders.\n18: \n19: Current state:\n20: \n21: - Shell scripts are the public command surface for spec lifecycle operations.\n22: - Validation delegates rule checks to `../rules/` and shared helpers in `../lib/`.\n23: - Scripts accept explicit spec folder paths and are intended to run from the repository root.\n24: \n25: ---\n26: \n27: ## 2. ARCHITECTURE\n28: \n29: ```text\n30: ╭──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮\n31: │                         SPEC SCRIPTS                         │\n32: ╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯\n33: \n34: ┌──────────────┐      ┌────────────────┐      ┌────────────────┐\n35: │ Operator     │ ───▶ │ create.sh      │ ───▶ │ Spec folder    │\n36: │ or command   │      │ upgrade-level  │      │ files          │\n37: └──────┬───────┘      └───────┬────────┘      └───────┬────────┘\n38:        │                      │                       │\n39:        │                      ▼                       ▼\n40:        │              ┌──────────────┐       ┌────────────────┐\n41:        └────────────▶ │ validate.sh  │ ───▶  │ rules/check-*  │\n42:                       └──────┬───────┘       └───────┬────────┘\n43:                              │                       │\n44:                              ▼                       ▼\n45:                       ┌──────────────┐       ┌────────────────┐\n46:                       │ completion   │       │ lib/*.sh       │\n47:                       │ and archive  │       │ helpers        │\n48:                       └──────────────┘       └────────────────┘\n49: \n50: Dependency direction: spec/*.sh ───▶ rules/*.sh ───▶ lib/*.sh\n51: ```\n52: \n53: ---\n54: \n55: ## 3. PACKAGE TOPOLOGY\n56: \n57: ```text\n58: runtime/cli/spec/\n59: +-- create.sh                    # Scaffold spec folders from templates\n60: +-- upgrade-level.sh             # Add level-owned docs and sections\n61: +-- check-placeholders.sh        # Detect unresolved template placeholders\n62: +-- validate.sh                  # Run structural validation rules\n63: +-- progressive-validate.sh      # Staged validation helper\n64: +-- check-completion.sh          # Verify the tasks checklist and acceptance closure\n65: +-- scaffold-debug-delegation.sh # Generate debug-delegation handoff scaffolds\n66: +-- calculate-completeness.sh    # Report checklist completion metrics\n67: +-- recommend-level.sh           # Recommend documentation level from task signals\n68: +-- archive.sh                   # Move completed or stale spec folders\n69: +-- check-template-staleness.sh  # Compare generated docs with templates\n70: +-- check-card-sync.sh           # Assert the authoring card, router, inventory and checklist agree\n71: +-- quality-audit.sh             # Batch quality audit helper\n72: +-- test-validation.sh           # Legacy wrapper for scripts/tests/test-validation.sh\n73: +-- is-phase-parent.ts           # Phase-parent detection and manifest health check\n74: +-- sync-phase-map-status.ts     # Sync a phase parent's map table; report completion mismatches\n75: +-- sweep-track-roots.mjs        # Report track roots whose children_ids differ from their packets\n76: +-- refresh-track-roots.mjs      # Rewrite a track root's children_ids to its packets on disk\n77: +-- repair-derived.cjs           # Repair packet facts derivable from disk; refuses authored facts\n78: +-- README-repair-derived.md     # Derived-vs-authored repair boundary reference\n79: +-- relocate.mjs                 # Move, renumber or re-parent packets and update every store\n80: +-- suggest-subtracks.mjs        # Propose a sub-track regrouping for a flat track, read-only\n81: +-- upgrade-legacy.mjs           # Upgrade a v3.x specs tree: repair failing packets, record the rest\n82: +-- template-phrase-census.mjs   # Read-only census of template trigger-phrase blocks\n83: +-- template-phrase-cleanup.mjs  # Rewrite template-seeded trigger phrases, dry run by default\n84: +-- template-phrase-lint.mjs     # Check trigger phrases a commit adds to staged Markdown\n85: +-- repo-era.mjs                 # Read-only Repo Era Report for a checkout\n86: `-- README.md\n87: ```\n88: \n89: Allowed direction:\n90: \n91: - `spec/*.sh` may source shared shell helpers from `../lib/`.\n92: - `validate.sh` may call validation rules from `../rules/`.\n93: - Lifecycle scripts may read templates and write only the selected spec folder. There are two exceptions. `create.sh --track` also lists the new packet in that track root's `children_ids`, and `upgrade-legacy.mjs` writes across every failing packet in the specs roots it is given.\n94: \n95: Disallowed direction:\n96: \n97: - Rule scripts should not mutate spec content.\n98: - Shell helpers should not call spec lifecycle entrypoints.\n99: - Source modules should not import generated `dist/` output directly. Shell entrypoints may execute freshness-verified compiled output, as `validate.sh` does for the runtime validation orchestrator.\n100: \n101: ---\n102: \n103: ## 4. KEY FILES\n104: \n105: | File | Role |\n106: |---|---|\n107: | `create.sh` | Creates new Level 1 or phase folders from templates. It seeds the trigger phrases of the 15 template document kinds with the packet slug. After the documents are written, it replaces the planned `graph-metadata.json` stub with metadata derived from them through `backfill-graph-metadata.ts`, and a failed derivation warns and leaves the stub in place. With `--track`, it then runs `refresh-track-roots.mjs` for that track, so the new packet is listed in the track root's `children_ids` from the start. |\n108: | `upgrade-level.sh` | Adds missing files and sections for higher documentation levels. |\n109: | `validate.sh` | Runs the modular validation gate used before completion claims. `resolve_orchestrator()` checks the compiled runtime dist freshness (via `../lib/dist-freshness.cjs`) before trusting it and fails closed with exit `3` when stale: no silent auto-rebuild. A linked `upgrade-baseline.json` is not applied: the validator warns `UPGRADE_BASELINE_LINK` and the findings it would have downgraded stay errors. `--quiet` prints only the result line. `--include-archive` needs `--recursive`, and exits `2` without it. It also validates the packets in the `z_archive/` of the folder given and, for a track root, of each declared sub-track. On a `--strict` run, a Draft packet whose only errors are derived-metadata rules gets one `repair-derived.cjs --apply` and one revalidation, so the run can write files. `SPECKIT_DRAFT_AUTO_REPAIR=0` (or a set `CI`, or a `SPECKIT_RULES` subset) turns that off. |\n110: | `check-completion.sh` | Confirms the `tasks.md` checklist evidence an","truncated":true}
{"type":"tool_result","callId":"chatcmpl-tool-9144d110ec9f51e8","status":"completed","result":"<path>/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Skilled/.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/README.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n610: ---\n611: \n612: ## 9. VERIFICATION\n613: \n614: | Check | How to run it |\n615: |---|---|\n616: | README structure | `python3 .skilled/skills/sk-doc/scripts/validate_document.py .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/README.md --type readme` reports zero issues |\n617: | Human Voice Rules | the em dash, semicolon and Oxford comma greps return zero prose hits |\n618: | Link resolution | the link guard reports no failures in this README |\n619: | Spec folder validation | `validate.sh` on a spec folder exits 0 |\n620: | Retrieval health | the trigger lookup exits `0` for a known phrase, and `runtime/data/trigger-index.json` parses |\n621: \n622: ### Scripts That Manage Spec Folders\n623: \n624: | Script | Purpose |\n625: |---|---|\n626: | `create.sh` | create spec folders with level-appropriate templates, `--phase` for parent plus child folders |\n627: | `validate.sh` | run the validation set from the 42-rule registry, `--strict` for strict-only rules, `--recursive` for phase folders, `--include-archive` with `--recursive` to also validate packets in the `z_archive/` of the folder given and, for a track root, of each declared sub-track, `--verbose` for details, `--quiet` for the result line only. A `--strict` run on a Draft packet whose only errors are derived metadata repairs it once and revalidates, so it can write files, and `SPECKIT_DRAFT_AUTO_REPAIR=0` turns that off |\n628: | `upgrade-level.sh` | render additional Level contract sections for a higher level |\n629: | `recommend-level.sh` | analyze scope and risk to recommend the right level |\n630: | `calculate-completeness.sh` | calculate spec folder completeness as a percentage |\n631: | `check-completion.sh` | verify all completion criteria are met |\n632: | `check-placeholders.sh` | find remaining `[YOUR_VALUE_HERE: ...]` tokens after a level upgrade |\n633: | `check-example-drift.sh` | report an example whose anchors or description drifted from its template |\n634: | `check-card-sync.sh` | report a disagreement between the authoring card, the router, the leaf inventory and the checklist |\n635: | `check-template-staleness.sh` | detect templates that need regeneration |\n636: | `progressive-validate.sh` | progressive validation for in-progress work |\n637: | `quality-audit.sh` | run a quality audit on spec folder content |\n638: | `archive.sh` | archive completed spec folders into the `z_archive/` beside them, which for a packet in a declared sub-track is the sub-track's own `z_archive/`. `--restore` returns a folder to the place its archive belongs to, and when a live packet already holds its number it lands on the next free number, counting archived packets, through `relocate.mjs`. After an archive or a plain restore the description cache `specs/descriptions.json` is rebuilt when it exists |\n639: | `relocate.mjs` | move, renumber or re-parent spec packets and rewrite every store that records the path, from `--map <file.json>`, `--from` with `--to`, `--renumber <folder>` or `--declare-subtrack <track>/<name>`. Dry run by default, `--apply` writes |\n640: | `suggest-subtracks.mjs` | propose a whole-track sub-track map for a flat track, or with `--describe` name the declared sub-track a new packet fits best. Prints the proposal and writes nothing |\n641: | `test-validation.sh` | test the validation rules themselves |\n642: \n643: ### Scripts That Maintain Continuity\n644: \n645: | Script | Purpose |\n646: |---|---|\n647: | `generate-context.ts` | source for the runtime entry point `runtime/cli/dist/continuity/generate-context.js` |\n648: | `backfill-frontmatter.ts` | add missing frontmatter to generated context artifacts |\n649: | `backfill-research-metadata.ts` | backfill missing metadata files under `research/*/iterations/` |\n650: | `validate-memory-quality.ts` | run quality checks on continuity content |\n651: \n652: TypeScript sources compile to `runtime/cli/dist/`.\n653: \n654: ### Validation Helper Scripts\n655: \n656: | Script | Purpose |\n657: |---|---|\n658: | `continuity-freshness.ts` | warn when `_memory.continuity.last_updated_at` lags `graph-metadata.json` |\n659: | `evidence-marker-audit.ts` | standalone CLI run on demand: a read-only bracket-depth report on `EVIDENCE` markers, and `--rewrap` to repair malformed closers. No validator calls it |\n660: \n661: The manual testing playbook runs every scenario behind these checks.\n662: \n663: ---\n664: \n665: ## 10. RELATED DOCUMENTS\n666: \n667: ### Internal Documentation\n668: \n669: | Document | Purpose |\n\n(Showing lines 610-669 of 716. Use offset=670 to continue.)\n</content>"}
