{"type":"tool_call","callId":"chatcmpl-tool-b11993fed1d23b0f","tool":"read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Skilled/.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/manual-testing-playbook/tooling-and-scripts/spec-packet-relocation.md"}}
{"type":"tool_result","callId":"chatcmpl-tool-8703559a00a2703d","status":"completed","result":"<path>/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Skilled/.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/feature-catalog/tooling-and-scripts/spec-packet-relocation.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Spec packet relocation\"\n3: description: \"Moves, renumbers and re-parents spec packets and rewrites every store that records the packet path, as a dry run unless --apply is passed.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"Spec packet relocation\"\n6:   - \"relocate.mjs\"\n7:   - \"move or renumber a spec packet\"\n8:   - \"declare a sub-track\"\n9:   - \"re-parent packets into a sub-track\"\n10: version: 1.0.0.0\n11: ---\n12: \n13: # Spec packet relocation (relocate.mjs)\n14: \n15: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n16: \n17: Moves, renumbers and re-parents spec packets and rewrites every store that records the packet path, as a dry run unless --apply is passed.\n18: \n19: A packet's path is written in many places, so a folder moved by hand leaves most of them naming the old place. `relocate.mjs` owns the order of operations and drives the writer that already owns each store. It is the tool an operator runs to regroup a track into sub-tracks, and the tool `archive.sh` calls when a restored packet needs a new number.\n20: \n21: ---\n22: \n23: ## 2. HOW IT WORKS\n24: \n25: ### Modes\n26: \n27: The tool runs from the repository root and takes exactly one mode. Paths are relative to the specs root, and a leading `specs/` is accepted.\n28: \n29: | Mode | What it does |\n30: |---|---|\n31: | `--map <file.json>` | Applies a list of `{ \"from\", \"to\" }` moves. A destination in a sub-track that does not exist yet declares that sub-track in the same run |\n32: | `--from <path> --to <path>` | Moves one packet. Both flags are needed together |\n33: | `--renumber <folder>` | Closes the number gaps among a folder's numbered children in their current order. Numbers held in that folder's `z_archive/` count as taken |\n34: | `--declare-subtrack <track>/<name>` | Declares a sub-track in a track that holds no flat packets |\n35: \n36: `--apply` writes. Without it the run prints every move and every file it would change and writes nothing. `--help` prints the usage line and exits 0.\n37: \n38: ### What a move rewrites\n39: \n40: An applied plan runs in a fixed order, and each step prints `ok` or `FAILED`:\n41: \n42: 1. New sub-tracks are declared: a `description.json` with level `sub-track` and a `z_archive/.gitkeep`.\n43: 2. Each folder moves, through `git mv` when git tracks it and a plain rename when it does not.\n44: 3. References to the old path are rewritten in the text files under `specs/`, and a phase parent's `children_ids` drops or adds the moved child. An old id is matched as a whole path, so a longer id that starts with it is left alone.\n45: 4. The moved packets' own recorded paths are re-derived: `description.json` through the generated-JSON migration, then graph ids and document packet pointers through `repair-derived.cjs`. Packets whose references changed are re-derived too.\n46: 5. The affected track and sub-track roots are refreshed through `refresh-track-roots.mjs`.\n47: 6. The moves are recorded in the relocation ledger, the citation redirect table is rebuilt and the trigger index is regenerated.\n48: \n49: The run then closes with `sweep-track-roots.mjs` and `validate.sh --strict` on each moved packet, and prints each packet's result line last.\n50: \n51: Code and CI files are never rewritten. A moved path named in the compiled route layout or in a workflow file under `.github/workflows` refuses the run, and the message names the file and line to update by hand.\n52: \n53: ### Refusals and exit codes\n54: \n55: Every refusal happens before the first write, each is printed as `relocate: refused: ...` and the run ends with `nothing was written`. The tool refuses:\n56: \n57: - a path outside `specs/` or one that passes through a symbolic link\n58: - a source or destination name that is not `NNN-slug`\n59: - a destination that already exists, or a number a live or archived sibling already holds\n60: - a source or destination named by two entries, a chain or swap between entries, or a destination inside its own source\n61: - a destination deeper than one sub-track, or in a folder that exists without the sub-track declaration\n62: - a plan that would leave a track holding flat packets beside sub-tracks\n63: - a tracked source with uncommitted changes, or an uncommitted file the run would rewrite\n64: - `--declare-subtrack` on a track without `graph-metadata.json`, on a folder that holds `spec.md` or on a track that still holds flat packets\n65: \n66: Exit status 0 means nothing is left to do, and after `--apply` it means every step landed and the close passed. Status 1 means a dry run found work. Status 2 means a rejected argument, a refused plan, a failed step or a failed close.\n67: \n68: The run is safe to repeat. An entry whose folder already sits at its destination is checked for stale records only, a second `--apply` of an applied map finds nothing to do, and a run that stopped on a failed step is finished by running the same map again.\n69: \n70: ---\n71: \n72: ## 3. SOURCE FILES\n73: \n74: ### Implementation\n75: \n76: | File | Layer | Role |\n77: |---|---|---|\n78: | `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/relocate.mjs` | Script | Parses the mode, checks the plan, moves folders, rewrites references and drives the store writers |\n79: | `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/lib/track-roots.mjs` | Shared | Reads tracks and sub-tracks and decides whether a track layout is mixed |\n80: | `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/repair-derived.cjs` | Script | Re-derives graph ids and packet pointers for moved packets and packets whose references changed |\n81: | `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/graph/migrate-generated-json.ts` | Script | Rewrites the path identity in each moved packet's `description.json` |\n82: | `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/refresh-track-roots.mjs` | Script | Refreshes the track and sub-track children lists the moves change |\n83: | `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/retrieval/generate-trigger-index.mjs` | Script | Regenerates the trigger index after the moves |\n84: | `.skilled/skills/sk-doc/shared/scripts/cite-drift-scan.mjs` | Script | Rebuilds the citation redirect table from the relocation ledger |\n85: | `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/archive.sh` | Script | Calls the tool to restore an archived packet onto the next free number |\n86: \n87: ### Validation And Tests\n88: \n89: | File | Type | Role |\n90: |---|---|---|\n91: | `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/tests/relocate.vitest.ts` | Vitest | Covers move, renumber, re-parent, sub-track declaration, the refusals, the dry-run exit status and a repeated apply |\n92: \n93: ---\n94: \n95: ## 4. SOURCE METADATA\n96: \n97: - Group: Tooling And Scripts\n98: - Canonical catalog source: `feature-catalog.md`\n99: - Feature file path: `tooling-and-scripts/spec-packet-relocation.md`\n100: \n101: Related references:\n102: - [sub-track-proposal.md](sub-track-proposal.md) - Prints the map this tool applies.\n103: - [spec-lifecycle-automation.md](spec-lifecycle-automation.md) - Covers the archive restore that calls this tool.\n104: - [track-root-children-lists.md](track-root-children-lists.md) - Covers the children lists this tool refreshes.\n\n(End of file - total 104 lines)\n</content>"}
{"type":"tool_result","callId":"chatcmpl-tool-b11993fed1d23b0f","status":"completed","result":"<path>/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Skilled/.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/manual-testing-playbook/tooling-and-scripts/spec-packet-relocation.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: title: \"492 -- Spec packet relocation and renumbering\"\n3: description: \"This scenario validates spec packet relocation for `492`. It focuses on the relocate.mjs dry run, a refused move onto an archived number and an applied renumber, all inside a throwaway copy of the checkout.\"\n4: version: 1.0.0.1\n5: ---\n6: \n7: # 492 -- Spec packet relocation and renumbering\n8: \n9: This document captures the throwaway-copy manual validation contract for `relocate.mjs`.\n10: \n11: ---\n12: \n13: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n14: \n15: This scenario validates spec packet relocation for `492`. It focuses on the `relocate.mjs` dry run, a refused move onto an archived number and an applied renumber. Every command runs inside a throwaway copy of the checkout under the operating-system temp directory. It never moves or rewrites anything in this checkout's `specs/` tree.\n16: \n17: ### Why This Matters\n18: \n19: A packet's path is recorded in its own `description.json` and `graph-metadata.json`, in other packets' references, in the track root and in the relocation ledger. A folder moved by hand leaves most of those naming the old place. The tool is dry by default and refuses before the first write, so an operator can read the whole plan before anything changes.\n20: \n21: ---\n22: \n23: ## 2. SCENARIO CONTRACT\n24: \n25: Operators run the exact prompt and command sequence for `492` and confirm each outcome without contradictory evidence.\n26: \n27: - Objective: Prove that `relocate.mjs` prints a complete plan and writes nothing without `--apply`, refuses a destination number that an archived packet holds, and moves the folders when `--apply` is passed in a throwaway copy.\n28: - Real user request: `The demo track has gaps in its packet numbers. Show me what a renumber would change before anything moves, then apply it in a throwaway copy and tell me where the packets ended up.`\n29: - Prompt: `Renumber the demo track with relocate.mjs in a throwaway copy of this checkout. Show the dry run first, confirm a move onto the archived number 002 is refused, then apply the renumber and report where each packet landed. Return PASS or FAIL with the copy path and the exit codes.`\n30: - Expected execution process: Copy the checkout without its `specs/` tree into the operating-system temp directory, build a small `demo` track there, commit it, run the dry runs and the refused move, apply the renumber, list the folders and rerun the dry run.\n31: - Expected signals: The dry runs exit `1` and print `relocate: dry run, nothing written (pass --apply to write)`. The refused move exits `2` and prints `relocate: nothing was written`. The applied run prints `relocate: applied 2 move(s)`, and the rerun prints `relocate: no moves to make; nothing to do` with exit `0`.\n32: - Desired user-visible outcome: A concise `PASS` with the copy path and the exit codes, or a `FAIL` that names the first violated check.\n33: - Pass/fail: PASS if every condition in Section 3 Pass / Fail holds, FAIL if any one does not.\n34: \n35: ---\n36: \n37: ## 3. TEST EXECUTION\n38: \n39: ### Prompt\n40: \n41: ```text\n42: Renumber the demo track with relocate.mjs in a throwaway copy of this checkout. Show the dry run first, confirm a move onto the archived number 002 is refused, then apply the renumber and report where each packet landed. Return PASS or FAIL with the copy path and the exit codes.\n43: ```\n44: \n45: ### Commands\n46: \n47: 1. From the repository root, copy the checkout without its `specs/` tree and its git data into a fresh temp directory. Confirm the printed path begins with the operating-system temp directory and is not the repository root. The copy carries the tooling because an applied run also rebuilds the trigger index, and that writer resolves its repository from the script's own location.\n48: \n49:    ```bash\n50:    export RELOCATE_REPO=\"$(pwd -P)\"\n51:    export RELOCATE_COPY=\"$(cd \"$(mktemp -d \"${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/relocate-manual.XXXXXX\")\" && pwd -P)\"\n52:    rsync -a --exclude .git --exclude specs \"$RELOCATE_REPO/\" \"$RELOCATE_COPY/\"\n53:    printf 'RELOCATE_COPY=%s\\n' \"$RELOCATE_COPY\"\n54:    ```\n55: \n56: 2. Build the fixture track inside the copy with `create.sh` and `archive.sh`. It holds three live packets numbered `001`, `004` and `006`, and one archived packet numbered `002`. The first packet names `specs/demo/004-beta` in its text. `git init` comes first, so the copy is its own git work tree before the scripts run. Do not write the packets by hand. A hand-written `graph-metadata.json` with no `manual` or `derived` object stops step 7 at the identity step with `Invalid graph metadata` and `relocate: stopped`.\n57: \n58:    ```bash\n59:    cd \"$RELOCATE_COPY\"\n60:    mkdir -p specs/demo\n61:    printf '{\"level\":\"track\",\"specFolder\":\"demo\",\"description\":\"Fixture track.\"}\\n' > specs/demo/description.json\n62:    printf '{\"schema_version\":1,\"packet_id\":\"demo\",\"spec_folder\":\"demo\",\"parent_id\":null,\"children_ids\":[]}\\n' > specs/demo/graph-metadata.json\n63:    git init --quiet\n64:    for p in 001-alpha 004-beta 006-gamma 002-old; do bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/create.sh --path specs/demo/$p --level 1 \"Fixture packet $p\" > /dev/null; done\n65:    bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/archive.sh --force specs/demo/002-old\n66:    printf '\\nFollow-up work lives in specs/demo/004-beta.\\n' >> specs/demo/001-alpha/spec.md\n67:    ```\n68: \n69: 3. Commit the fixture. The tool refuses a run that would rewrite a file with uncommitted changes, and an untracked file counts as one.\n70: \n71:    ```bash\n72:    cd \"$RELOCATE_COPY\" && git add -f specs\n73:    git -c commit.gpgsign=false -c user.name=Fixture -c user.email=fixture@example.com commit --quiet --no-verify -m fixture\n74:    ```\n75: \n76: 4. Dry-run the renumber of the whole track.\n77: \n78:    ```bash\n79:    cd \"$RELOCATE_COPY\" && node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/relocate.mjs --renumber demo\n80:    echo \"RENUMBER_DRY_EXIT=$?\"\n81:    ```\n82: \n83: 5. Dry-run a single move with `--from` and `--to`.\n84: \n85:    ```bash\n86:    cd \"$RELOCATE_COPY\" && node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/relocate.mjs --from demo/006-gamma --to demo/005-gamma\n87:    echo \"MOVE_DRY_EXIT=$?\"\n88:    ```\n89: \n90: 6. Ask for a move onto the number the archived packet holds, then list the track to confirm nothing moved.\n91: \n92:    ```bash\n93:    cd \"$RELOCATE_COPY\" && node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/relocate.mjs --from demo/006-gamma --to demo/002-gamma\n94:    echo \"REFUSED_EXIT=$?\"\n95:    ls specs/demo specs/demo/z_archive\n96:    ```\n97: \n98: 7. Apply the renumber inside the copy, then list the track again.\n99: \n100:    ```bash\n101:    cd \"$RELOCATE_COPY\" && node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/relocate.mjs --renumber demo --apply\n102:    echo \"APPLY_EXIT=$?\"\n103:    ls specs/demo specs/demo/z_archive\n104:    ```\n105: \n106: 8. Rerun the dry run. A track with no gap left has no moves to make.\n107: \n108:    ```bash\n109:    cd \"$RELOCATE_COPY\" && node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/relocate.mjs --renumber demo\n110:    echo \"RERUN_EXIT=$?\"\n111:    ```\n112: \n113: 9. Remove the copy.\n114: \n115:    ```bash\n116:    cd \"$RELOCATE_REPO\" && rm -rf \"$RELOCATE_COPY\"\n117:    ```\n118: \n119: ### Expected\n120: \n121: Step 4 prints this plan and `RENUMBER_DRY_EXIT=1`. The live packet after the gap moves to `003`, not `002`, because the archived packet still owns `002`.\n122: \n123: ```text\n124: relocate: dry run, nothing written (pass --apply to write)\n125:   move     specs/demo/004-beta -> specs/demo/003-beta\n126:   move     specs/demo/006-gamma -> specs/demo/004-gamma\n127:   rewrite  specs/demo/001-alpha/spec.md: 1 reference(s)\n128:   rewrite  specs/demo/003-beta/description.json (moved): 1 reference(s)\n129:   rewrite  specs/demo/003-beta/graph-metadata.json (moved): 2 reference(s)\n130:   rewrite  specs/demo/004-gamma/description.json (moved): 1 reference(s)\n131:   rewrite  specs/demo/004-gamma/graph-metadata.json (moved):","truncated":true}
