{"type":"thinking","text":"Confirmed:\n- rg-wrapper.mjs line 380-382: `record.archivedPaths = record.paths.filter(isArchivedPath)`. The feature catalog line 28 explicitly says \"The `paths` list of the path-only recipe stays a list of plain strings.\" That claim can be read as only `paths` remains strings (true), but phase 009 adds `archivedPaths`; the sentence \"Without the flag none of these fields appear, and the report shape is unchanged\" and no mention of archivedPaths means the catalog is stale/incomplete. The parent spec completion criterion: \"the `--json` path record marks the same paths\" — that's `archivedPaths`. So the catalog should document it. This is a P1/P2 gap.\n\n- suggest-subtracks line 296: refuses non-NNN-slug packet with a message \"must be renamed first\" (exit 0 explained non-answer? need to check). Line 153: proposed group may not reuse an existing folder. The feature catalog says line 28 \"Archived packets are not counted or mapped\" and line 30 explains non-answers but doesn't mention the non-NNN-slug refusal or existing folder name collision. Stale.\n\n- relocate case-fold: lines 472-488 confirm duplicate check folds case. Feature catalog refusals list doesn't mention this; it says \"a source or destination named by two entries\" (line 60). Two case spellings of one folder is a distinct refusal. Stale/incomplete.\n\n- phase-map bare-name and pre-archive: relocate lines 651-654 confirm \"The id a packet had before it was archived\". The feature catalog line 44 says \"An old id is matched as a whole path, so a longer id that starts with it is left alone.\" and line 47 step 3 doesn't mention phase map bare names. Stale.\n\nNow let me check the changelog v2.13.0.0.md, which is likely about phase 001-005 and not updated for 006-010. Also SKILL.md (no relocate mention at all — is that a gap? The SKILL.md is the skill description; it might reasonably not list every script; but the review focus says \"code against .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled\" and docs against sk-create-* standards. For a skill SKILL.md, should describe the tool surface. The system-spec-kit SKILL.md mention \"Scripts: runtime/cli/spec/validate.sh, create.sh...\" at line 317, omitting relocate/suggest/archive. That's a discoverability gap: the SKILL.md never mentions the relocation engine, subtrack proposal, or archive search flags. The focus explicitly says \"SKILL.md ... never updated\". This is a real finding: SKILL.md's tool table (line 285-297) doesn't include relocate/suggest-subtracks/archive/search archive; so an agent reading the skill won't know the new capability. Severity P1? It's documentation/traceability of a new feature; the skill's job is to route agents; omission means the capability is undiscoverable. I'd say P1 (incomplete implementation? docs) or P2. Hmm. The objective's completion criteria include documentation updates? The phase 002 scope says \"Raise the subtrack choice ... in ... the structure docs\" and phase 001-004 docs. The fact that docs never updated for 006-010 is the operator's stated highest-yield issue. The review should find these as P1/P2 with exact lines. There may be multiple findings across docs. We need a good set, not necessarily one per behavior.\n\nLet me read the changelog and the references."}
{"type":"tool_call","callId":"chatcmpl-tool-ac63de2eaeac970c","tool":"read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Skilled/.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/changelog/v2.13.0.0.md"}}
{"type":"tool_result","callId":"chatcmpl-tool-ac63de2eaeac970c","status":"completed","result":"<path>/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Skilled/.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/changelog/v2.13.0.0.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: title: \"system-spec-kit v2.13.0.0, packets move by command and archives can be searched\"\n3: description: \"One command moves, renumbers or re-parents a spec packet and fixes every record of its path. A new tool proposes sub-tracks and every sub-track gets its own archive. Search and validation can reach archived packets on request.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"system-spec-kit v2.13.0.0\"\n6:   - \"system-spec-kit 2.13.0.0\"\n7:   - \"move a spec packet\"\n8:   - \"propose sub-tracks\"\n9:   - \"search archived packets\"\n10: importance_tier: \"normal\"\n11: contextType: \"general\"\n12: version: 2.13.0.0\n13: ---\n14: # v2.13.0.0, Packets Move by Command and Archives Can Be Searched\n15: \n16: Moving a spec packet meant `git mv` and hand edits. Several records still named the old path afterwards. This release adds one command that moves a packet and fixes every record.\n17: \n18: A track could grow into one long flat list. A new tool reads a track and proposes sub-tracks (named groups inside a track). It only prints. The spec-folder question now offers a sub-track too.\n19: \n20: Archives were half supported. Search skipped them and recursive validation never entered them. Numbering ignored them, so a new packet could take an archived number. Each gap is closed. The archive search and validation are opt-in.\n21: \n22: > Spec folder: `specs/system-speckit/spec-folder-tooling/005-subtrack-proposal-and-archive-reach`\n23: \n24: &nbsp;\n25: \n26: ## Why This Release\n27: \n28: A packet's path is written in many places. Its own metadata holds it. So do parent lists, track roots, links in other packets, the description cache and the trigger index. A hand move left most of them stale. Renumbering had the same cost.\n29: \n30: Sub-tracks existed only as a hand-written declaration. No tool suggested one and no tool created one. An archived packet was out of reach for search and for validation, with no switch to change that.\n31: \n32: &nbsp;\n33: \n34: ## What's New at a Glance\n35: \n36: - **One command moves a packet.** `relocate.mjs` moves, renumbers and re-parents packets. It rewrites every record of the path. <!-- section: Moving Packets -->\n37: - **A move is a dry run first.** The run prints its plan and writes nothing. `--apply` writes. <!-- section: Moving Packets -->\n38: - **A tool proposes sub-tracks.** `suggest-subtracks.mjs` groups a flat track's packets and prints a map. It writes nothing. <!-- section: Sub-Tracks -->\n39: - **The spec-folder question offers a sub-track.** Option C names an existing sub-track or a new one. `create.sh` prints one hint line. <!-- section: Sub-Tracks -->\n40: - **Every sub-track has its own archive.** `archive.sh` moves a sub-track's packet into that sub-track's `z_archive/`. <!-- section: Archives -->\n41: - **Numbering counts archived packets.** A new packet never takes an archived number. A restore onto a taken number picks the next free one. <!-- section: Archives -->\n42: - **Search can reach archives.** `rg-wrapper.mjs --include-archive` also searches archived packets. Each such hit is marked `[archived]`. <!-- section: Archive Search and Validation -->\n43: - **Validation can reach archives.** `validate.sh --recursive --include-archive` also checks archived packets. <!-- section: Archive Search and Validation -->\n44: \n45: &nbsp;\n46: \n47: ## Moving Packets\n48: \n49: #### One Command Moves a Packet\n50: \n51: `relocate.mjs` takes one of four inputs. `--map <file.json>` moves many packets. `--from <path> --to <path>` moves one. `--renumber <folder>` gives a packet the next free number. `--declare-subtrack <track>/<name>` creates a sub-track. A map is a list of pairs:\n52: \n53: ```json\n54: [{ \"from\": \"<track>/NNN-slug\", \"to\": \"<track>/<sub>/NNN-slug\" }]\n55: ```\n56: \n57: On `--apply` it moves each folder, with `git mv` when git tracks it. It rewrites every mention of the old path under `specs/` and recomputes relative links. Then it runs the tools that own each record. Those cover packet metadata, track roots and the trigger index. Each move is added to a committed ledger. The run ends with a sweep and a strict validation of each moved packet.\n58: \n59: #### A Dry Run Comes First\n60: \n61: Without `--apply` the run prints every move and every file it would change. A real dry run ends like this:\n62: \n63: ```text\n64: moves=1 moved=0 rewrites=33 identity=1 roots=3 ledger=1\n65: ```\n66: \n67: Exit 0 means nothing is left to do. Exit 1 means a dry run found work. Exit 2 means the plan was refused or a step failed. A second `--apply` of the same map prints `relocate: nothing to do`. A map that stopped part-way finishes on the next run.\n68: \n69: #### Unsafe Plans Are Refused Early\n70: \n71: Every refusal comes before the first write, so a refused plan changes nothing. The run refuses a collision, a taken number and a bad name. It refuses a track left with both flat packets and sub-tracks. It refuses a second sub-track level, a path outside `specs/` and a path through a link. It also refuses uncommitted work on a path it would touch. Each refusal prints a `refused:` line.\n72: \n73: #### Code and CI Paths Are Reported\n74: \n75: A moved path can be hard-coded in code or in a CI workflow. `relocate.mjs` never rewrites those files. It names each file and line and exits 2. Fix them by hand, then run the map again.\n76: \n77: #### Smaller Fixes\n78: \n79: - **A map that names one source twice is refused.** Such a map passed the checks. The apply step moved the first entry and failed on the second. The tree was left half moved.\n80: - **A packet moved to a flat track loses its old parent.** `repair-derived.cjs` kept the parent recorded before the move. It now clears it.\n81: - **More documents get their path pointer healed.** `repair-derived.cjs` now also fixes `goal.md`, `acceptance-criteria.md`, `resource-map.md` and `decision-record.md`.\n82: \n83: &nbsp;\n84: \n85: ## Sub-Tracks\n86: \n87: #### A Tool Proposes the Groups\n88: \n89: Run `suggest-subtracks.mjs --track <track>` on a flat track. It reads each packet's `description.json` and `graph-metadata.json`. It drops words that most packets share, since those describe the whole track. It groups packets that share enough of the rest. Each group is named from its two most shared words. Packets that match nothing go to a sub-track named `general`.\n90: \n91: The map names every live packet once, in the format `relocate.mjs --map` accepts. A track under 8 live packets gets a reason instead:\n92: \n93: ```text\n94: track cli-classifier has 6 live packets, below the threshold of 8; no map\n95: ```\n96: \n97: `--threshold <n>` changes that number. The tool never writes. Moving stays with `relocate.mjs` after you approve the map.\n98: \n99: #### It Places a New Packet\n100: \n101: Add `--describe \"<text>\"` on a track that already has sub-tracks. The tool scores the text against each one. A fit needs at least 2 matched words. The tool names the best fit or says none fits:\n102: \n103: ```text\n104: best fit: system-speckit/spec-folder-tooling\n105: ```\n106: \n107: #### The Question Offers a Sub-Track\n108: \n109: Option C of the spec-folder question now includes a sub-track of the track. That can be an existing one. It can also be a new one, proposed as a map for the whole track.\n110: \n111: `create.sh` adds one hint line on stderr in three cases. The track has grown into clusters. The packet fits no existing sub-track. The packet fits another sub-track better than the one you named. The line never stops the scaffold:\n112: \n113: ```text\n114: [speckit] Track demo has grown into clusters. Consider sub-tracks: node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/suggest-subtracks.mjs --track demo\n115: ```\n116: \n117: #### A Sub-Track Can Be Declared\n118: \n119: `relocate.mjs --declare-subtrack <track>/<name> --apply` creates a sub-track. It writes the description, the graph metadata and `z_archive/.gitkeep`. It refuses a track that still holds flat packets, since that would leave the track mixed. A map that names a new sub-track declares it in the same run.\n120: \n121: &nbsp;\n122: \n123: ## Archives\n124: \n125: ####","truncated":true}
{"type":"tool_call","callId":"chatcmpl-tool-b73570a18547e6be","tool":"bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Skilled && grep -n \"relocate\\|sub-track\\|subtrack\\|z_archive\\|include-archive\\|archive\" .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/phase-definitions.md .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/workflows/quick-reference.md .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/workflows/worked-examples.md | head -80","description":"Grep references for relocation and archive content"}}
{"type":"tool_result","callId":"chatcmpl-tool-b73570a18547e6be","status":"completed","result":".skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:99:A track may hold one optional level of named sub-tracks between itself and its packets:\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:104:├── <sub-track>/\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:105:│   ├── 001-<slug>/                # numbering restarts inside the sub-track\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:107:└── z_archive/\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:110:A sub-track is declared, not inferred. All of these hold:\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:115:| `description.json` | `\"level\": \"sub-track\"` and `specFolder` set to `<track>/<sub-track>`. `specId` is not required: the description producers write only `specFolder`, and no reader derives a sub-track identity from `specId`. |\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:116:| `graph-metadata.json` | Track-root shape: `packet_id` `<track>/<sub-track>`, `parent_id` the track, `children_ids` its immediate numbered packets |\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:117:| Track registration | The sub-track id appears in the track's `graph-metadata.json` `children_ids` |\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:119:Numbering continues per folder, so each sub-track numbers its own packets from `001`. One level is supported: a sub-track holds numbered packets, not another sub-track. A declared folder that contains `spec.md` gets the diagnostic `declared folder has spec.md and cannot be a sub-track`, and `create.sh` refuses it.\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:121:A track's `children_ids` lists its direct numbered packets and its declared sub-tracks. A sub-track's `children_ids` lists only its own immediate numbered packets. A numbered phase parent counts as one entry, and its phases stay its own children.\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:123:A sub-track has its own `z_archive/`. Any `z_` folder inside a track or sub-track is never a sub-track and is never discovered as content. A non-numbered folder without the declaration gets one diagnostic and is never promoted. A symlinked sub-track is skipped with one diagnostic in both the working-tree and the commit view. The sweep treats these diagnostics as advisory and does not change its exit code. `create.sh` uses sub-track diagnostics to explain invalid `--track` values and exits with status 1.\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:127:A sub-track is a proposal the operator approves, never a move made silently. Propose one at two moments.\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:129:**When a packet is created.** `create.sh` prints one advisory line to stderr when the track has grown into clusters, when the new packet fits no existing sub-track, or when it fits a different sub-track better than the one named. The line never blocks the scaffold. Ask the same question directly with:\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:132:node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/suggest-subtracks.mjs --track <track> --describe \"<what the packet is for>\"\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:138:node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/suggest-subtracks.mjs --track <track> > map.json\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:141:For a flat track, the map names every live packet of the track exactly once, because a track holds either packets or sub-tracks and never both. The tool prints no map in three cases, each with one line and exit `0`: a track below the threshold, a track where no grouping is found, and a track that already has a declared sub-track.\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:143:A track that already has a declared sub-track gets `track <track> is already grouped into sub-tracks; no map`, also when numbered packets still sit beside the sub-tracks. For such a track, ask where each packet fits with `--describe`, which prints `best fit: <track>/<sub-track>` or `none fits: propose a new sub-track in <track>`. Then write the map by hand, one `{ \"from\": \"<track>/NNN-slug\", \"to\": \"<track>/<sub-track>/NNN-slug\" }` entry for every packet still at the track level, and run it through `relocate.mjs --map`. The map must cover all of them, because `relocate.mjs` refuses a plan that leaves flat packets beside sub-tracks. A single remaining packet can move with `relocate.mjs --from <path> --to <path>`.\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:145:Rename the candidate sub-track names when a clearer name exists, show the map to the operator, and apply the approved map with the relocation tool:\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:148:node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/relocate.mjs --map map.json          # dry run, writes nothing\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:149:node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/relocate.mjs --map map.json --apply\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:152:`relocate.mjs` declares each new sub-track, moves and renumbers the packets and rewrites every store that records a packet path. Do not move a packet by hand.\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:306:Completed or superseded specs use the `z_archive/` prefix:\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:312:└── z_archive/\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:317:A declared sub-track archives into its own `z_archive/`, not the track's:\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:323:│   └── z_archive/\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:325:└── z_archive/\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:418:├── invoicing/                       # declared sub-track\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:419:│   ├── description.json             # level: \"sub-track\"\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md:425:└── z_archive/\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/phase-definitions.md:72:A series parent groups changes to one artifact. Grouping a whole track's packets by theme is a sub-track instead, and [folder-structure.md](folder-structure.md) says when to propose one under \"When to Propose a Sub-Track\".\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/workflows/quick-reference.md:139:# In a declared sub-track, numbered from 001 inside it:\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/workflows/quick-reference.md:231:| `archived` | Historical record | 5 (do not reuse) |\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/workflows/quick-reference.md:267:**Sub-Tracks:** If the work belongs to a track that is grouped into sub-tracks, prefer Option C and place the packet in the sub-track that fits. If the track's packets have grown into clusters and no sub-track fits, prefer Option C and propose a new sub-track as a whole-track map (`folder-structure.md` §2, When to Propose a Sub-Track). A sub-track is a proposal the operator approves, never a move made silently.\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/workflows/quick-reference.md:279:- **Option C:** Use another existing packet — a related spec when it is a better scope match, a specific child under an existing phase parent, a related standard packet decomposed into phases when it meets both phase thresholds, a sub-track of the track (an existing one, or a new one proposed as a whole-track map when the track's packets have grown into clusters), or a series parent when the work is a different change to the same arti","truncated":true}
