{"type":"thinking","text":"The changelog is v2.13.0.0 (release of phases 001-005) and it explicitly states at line 67 only exit codes 0/1/2 (stale now with exit 3 from phase 008). Also line 221 says \"A stopped `--renumber` run does not finish on a rerun... Run repair-derived and refresh-track-roots on the moved packets by hand\" — phase 006 fixed exactly this for --map and possibly --renumber? The review focus says \"late-step ledger marks and resume\" and \"relocate.mjs exit 3\". The changelog's line 221 is now false (phase 006 fixed resume). That's a direct stale claim in the changelog. Since changelogs are historical records, should they be updated? The operator focus says changelog was never updated for phases 006-010. Changelogs usually aren't retroactively edited, but this one is version 2.13.0.0 for the whole release; phases 006-010 are fixes to the same release, so updating it is plausible. The focus explicitly lists changelog. The stale claim at line 221 is a P1/P2: tells operators to fix by hand, contradicting D3 and phase 006. That's a significant documentation error (it instructs manual repair which the decisions forbid). I'd rate P1.\n\nAlso line 67 exit codes stale (omits 3). P1 or P2.\n\nLine 165: \"With the flag, JSON rows carry `archived: true` or `archived: false`\" — phase 009 added archivedPaths for path recipes; not mentioned. P2.\nLine 89/91: suggest-subtracks \"Each group is named from its two most shared words\" — phase 009 added avoiding existing folder names; line 91 \"The map names every live packet once, in the format relocate accepts\" — but phase 009 fixed cases where it didn't (non-NNN-slug packets now refused; existing undeclared folder names avoided). The changelog doesn't mention. P2.\n\nNow the references: folder-structure.md is fairly updated for phase 002/009? It mentions suggest-subtracks and archive. Does it mention exit 3 / resume / pre-archive? Not likely. Let me read folder-structure around sub-track creation and archive. Also phase-definitions.md (bare-name phase map? re-parent rewrites?). The review focus mentions \"phase-map bare-name and re-parent rewrites\". The docs that should mention that are phase-definitions.md and worked-examples.md maybe. Let me read phase-definitions.md fully? It's ~? Let me check size. And quick-reference around lines 120-160 (track layout) and 260-290.\n\nLet me batch reads: folder-structure 90-160, 300-330; phase-definitions 1-140 maybe; quick-reference 120-160, 255-290."}
{"type":"tool_call","callId":"chatcmpl-tool-ad18c10fa4b1e350","tool":"read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Skilled/.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md","offset":90,"limit":80}}
{"type":"tool_result","callId":"chatcmpl-tool-ad18c10fa4b1e350","status":"completed","result":"<path>/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Skilled/.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/folder-structure.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n90: ```\n91: 007-feature/\n92: ├── 001-initial-implementation/\n93: ├── 002-bug-fixes/\n94: └── 003-performance-optimization/\n95: ```\n96: \n97: ### Sub-Track Folders\n98: \n99: A track may hold one optional level of named sub-tracks between itself and its packets:\n100: \n101: ```\n102: specs/<track>/\n103: ├── <NNN>-<slug>/                  # packet filed directly in the track\n104: ├── <sub-track>/\n105: │   ├── 001-<slug>/                # numbering restarts inside the sub-track\n106: │   └── 002-<slug>/\n107: └── z_archive/\n108: ```\n109: \n110: A sub-track is declared, not inferred. All of these hold:\n111: \n112: | Part | Requirement |\n113: |---|---|\n114: | Shape | Direct child of a track root, name not `NNN-*`, no `spec.md` |\n115: | `description.json` | `\"level\": \"sub-track\"` and `specFolder` set to `<track>/<sub-track>`. `specId` is not required: the description producers write only `specFolder`, and no reader derives a sub-track identity from `specId`. |\n116: | `graph-metadata.json` | Track-root shape: `packet_id` `<track>/<sub-track>`, `parent_id` the track, `children_ids` its immediate numbered packets |\n117: | Track registration | The sub-track id appears in the track's `graph-metadata.json` `children_ids` |\n118: \n119: Numbering continues per folder, so each sub-track numbers its own packets from `001`. One level is supported: a sub-track holds numbered packets, not another sub-track. A declared folder that contains `spec.md` gets the diagnostic `declared folder has spec.md and cannot be a sub-track`, and `create.sh` refuses it.\n120: \n121: A track's `children_ids` lists its direct numbered packets and its declared sub-tracks. A sub-track's `children_ids` lists only its own immediate numbered packets. A numbered phase parent counts as one entry, and its phases stay its own children.\n122: \n123: A sub-track has its own `z_archive/`. Any `z_` folder inside a track or sub-track is never a sub-track and is never discovered as content. A non-numbered folder without the declaration gets one diagnostic and is never promoted. A symlinked sub-track is skipped with one diagnostic in both the working-tree and the commit view. The sweep treats these diagnostics as advisory and does not change its exit code. `create.sh` uses sub-track diagnostics to explain invalid `--track` values and exits with status 1.\n124: \n125: ### When to Propose a Sub-Track\n126: \n127: A sub-track is a proposal the operator approves, never a move made silently. Propose one at two moments.\n128: \n129: **When a packet is created.** `create.sh` prints one advisory line to stderr when the track has grown into clusters, when the new packet fits no existing sub-track, or when it fits a different sub-track better than the one named. The line never blocks the scaffold. Ask the same question directly with:\n130: \n131: ```bash\n132: node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/suggest-subtracks.mjs --track <track> --describe \"<what the packet is for>\"\n133: ```\n134: \n135: **When a flat track has grown.** A flat track with 8 or more live packets is a candidate. `--threshold <n>` changes that number. The tool clusters the track's packets by the terms their `description.json` and `graph-metadata.json` share and prints a whole-track map:\n136: \n137: ```bash\n138: node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/suggest-subtracks.mjs --track <track> > map.json\n139: ```\n140: \n141: For a flat track, the map names every live packet of the track exactly once, because a track holds either packets or sub-tracks and never both. The tool prints no map in three cases, each with one line and exit `0`: a track below the threshold, a track where no grouping is found, and a track that already has a declared sub-track.\n142: \n143: A track that already has a declared sub-track gets `track <track> is already grouped into sub-tracks; no map`, also when numbered packets still sit beside the sub-tracks. For such a track, ask where each packet fits with `--describe`, which prints `best fit: <track>/<sub-track>` or `none fits: propose a new sub-track in <track>`. Then write the map by hand, one `{ \"from\": \"<track>/NNN-slug\", \"to\": \"<track>/<sub-track>/NNN-slug\" }` entry for every packet still at the track level, and run it through `relocate.mjs --map`. The map must cover all of them, because `relocate.mjs` refuses a plan that leaves flat packets beside sub-tracks. A single remaining packet can move with `relocate.mjs --from <path> --to <path>`.\n144: \n145: Rename the candidate sub-track names when a clearer name exists, show the map to the operator, and apply the approved map with the relocation tool:\n146: \n147: ```bash\n148: node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/relocate.mjs --map map.json          # dry run, writes nothing\n149: node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/relocate.mjs --map map.json --apply\n150: ```\n151: \n152: `relocate.mjs` declares each new sub-track, moves and renumbers the packets and rewrites every store that records a packet path. Do not move a packet by hand.\n153: \n154: ---\n155: \n156: ## 3. LEVEL REQUIREMENTS\n157: \n158: ### Level 1 (< 100 LOC)\n159: \n160: **Required Files:**\n161: ```\n162: specs/NNN-name/\n163: ├── spec.md                    # Problem statement, goals, scope\n164: ├── plan.md                    # Implementation approach\n165: ├── tasks.md                   # Task breakdown\n166: └── implementation-summary.md  # Created AFTER implementation completes\n167: ```\n168: \n169: **Optional:**\n\n(Showing lines 90-169 of 462. Use offset=170 to continue.)\n</content>"}
{"type":"tool_call","callId":"chatcmpl-tool-a4fcbe66279ea515","tool":"bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Skilled && wc -l .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/phase-definitions.md .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/workflows/quick-reference.md .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/workflows/worked-examples.md .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/retrieval/retrieval-conventions.md && grep -n \"spec.md\\|phase map\\|Phase Documentation\\|reparent\\|re-parent\\|renumber\\|folder name\\|bare\" .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/phase-definitions.md | head -30","description":"Check reference doc sizes and phase rewrite mentions"}}
{"type":"tool_result","callId":"chatcmpl-tool-a4fcbe66279ea515","status":"completed","result":"     303 .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/structure/phase-definitions.md\n     747 .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/workflows/quick-reference.md\n     319 .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/workflows/worked-examples.md\n     344 .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/retrieval/retrieval-conventions.md\n    1713 total\n80:Create the series parent at the second packet, not later, so each child's commit history stays on one path for as long as possible. Its slug names the artifact, for example `041-write-recipe-fixes`, and its `spec.md` names the artifact and the condition that closes the series. It keeps the lean trio plus an optional `timeline.md` and gets no `goal.md` by default.\n146:- **Lean trio:** A phase parent only requires **the lean trio** at the parent level: `spec.md`, `description.json`, `graph-metadata.json`.\n150:- **Parent spec map:** The parent's `spec.md` carries the Phase Documentation Map.\n155:├── spec.md                    # Vision, scope, Phase Documentation Map\n159:│   ├── spec.md\n171:**Detection rule (single source of truth):** A spec folder is a phase parent iff (a) it has at least one direct child matching `^[0-9]{3}-[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]*$` AND (b) at least one such child has `spec.md` OR `description.json`. The shell helper `is_phase_parent()` (in `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/lib/shell-common.sh`) and the ESM JS helper `isPhaseParent()` (in `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/dist/spec/is-phase-parent.js`) both implement this contract; they MUST agree on every input. The validator's phase-parent branches in `check-files.sh`, `check-level-match.sh`, `check-template-source.sh`, and the native `ANCHORS_VALID` rule in `runtime/lib/validation/orchestrator.ts` skip Level-N expectations when this returns true.\n173:**Parent template:** New phase decompositions via `create.sh --phase --phases N --phase-names a,b,c` scaffold the parent from `templates/packet-types/phase-parent.spec.md.tmpl` and each child through the manifest-backed Level contract. The lean template carries an inline content-discipline comment listing FORBIDDEN narrative tokens (consolidation, merge, migration history) and REQUIRED content (root purpose, sub-phase control file, what needs done). This is a template convention rather than a validated rule.\n177:### Parent spec.md: Phase Documentation Map\n179:The parent `spec.md` includes a Phase Documentation Map section that lists all child phases with their status:\n182:## Phase Documentation Map\n191:### Child spec.md: Parent Back-Reference\n193:Each child phase `spec.md` includes a metadata table linking it to the parent and adjacent phases:\n198:| **Parent Spec** | `../spec.md` |\n199:| **Predecessor** | `../NNN-previous-phase/spec.md` |\n200:| **Successor** | `../NNN-next-phase/spec.md` |\n219:       └── Phase Documentation Map defined in parent spec.md\n228:       └── Phase status updated in parent Phase Documentation Map\n255:2. Prefer Option C when the work is a distinct but related workstream within the parent's documented purpose and scope. Add the next sequential child phase and update the Phase Documentation Map. When the parent has a `goal.md`, give the new phase its child goal and binding row with `/create:goal <parent> phase-add`.\n257:4. Use Option B only when the work is new or unrelated to the phase parent's purpose, requirements, and Phase Documentation Map.\n"}
