{"type":"tool_call","callId":"chatcmpl-tool-8629385eb9ceb228","tool":"read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Skilled/.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/manual-testing-playbook/tooling-and-scripts/spec-lifecycle-automation.md"}}
{"type":"tool_result","callId":"chatcmpl-tool-bfaa75ba720eb879","status":"completed","result":"<path>/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Skilled/.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/feature-catalog/tooling-and-scripts/spec-lifecycle-automation.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Spec lifecycle automation\"\n3: description: \"Coordinated shell lifecycle for recommending spec depth, creating spec folders and phases, upgrading level, measuring completion and archiving finished work.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - spec lifecycle automation\n6:   - recommend-level.sh\n7:   - create.sh upgrade-level.sh\n8:   - spec folder archival\n9:   - completeness measurement\n10: version: 1.6.2.0\n11: ---\n12: \n13: # Spec Lifecycle Automation\n14: \n15: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n16: \n17: Spec Lifecycle Automation is the shell-script toolchain that moves Spec Kit work through its operational lifecycle instead of treating spec folders as static templates.\n18: \n19: The lifecycle is intentionally split into focused commands. `recommend-level.sh` scores a task and recommends the documentation level and optional phasing strategy. `create.sh` materializes the parent spec, optional child phases, or versioned subfolders. `upgrade-level.sh` raises an existing spec folder to a richer documentation level without discarding authored content. `calculate-completeness.sh` measures placeholder burn-down and optional content quality, `check-completion.sh` enforces checklist completion and evidence rules, and `archive.sh` archives or restores completed work with a completeness gate.\n20: \n21: Two newer tools sit beside these scripts and have their own entries. `relocate.mjs` moves, renumbers and re-parents packets and is described in [spec-packet-relocation.md](spec-packet-relocation.md). `suggest-subtracks.mjs` proposes a sub-track grouping and is described in [sub-track-proposal.md](sub-track-proposal.md). This entry covers only where the lifecycle scripts call them.\n22: \n23: ---\n24: \n25: ## 2. HOW IT WORKS\n26: \n27: ### Core Behavior\n28: \n29: The shipped lifecycle is modular rather than fully auto-chained.\n30: \n31: Level recommendation is handled by `recommend-level.sh` as a standalone scoring tool. It accepts estimated LOC, file count, and risk flags for auth, API, database, and architectural work, converts those inputs into a 100-point score, maps the result to Level 0 through Level 3, and separately computes whether phased execution is recommended. Phase scoring uses its own 50-point signal model and only recommends phases when the phase score crosses the threshold and the recommended documentation level is Level 3 or higher.\n32: \n33: Spec creation is handled by `create.sh`, which defaults to Level 1 unless `--level` is provided. The script creates `scratch/`, copies the level template set, optionally creates a git branch and writes `description.json` when the Node helper is available. For root and phase scaffolds it then derives `graph-metadata.json` through the graph backfill, which warns and continues when the derivation fails. It also replaces the template placeholder trigger phrases of 18 document kinds with phrases built from the packet slug, and it runs full post-create validation only when `SPECKIT_POST_VALIDATE=1` is set. Beyond normal folder creation, it supports versioned subfolders with `--subfolder`, phased parent-plus-child creation with `--phase`, append-mode phase growth with `--parent`, named child phases with `--phase-names`, and Level 3 sharded spec sections with `--sharded`. The current implementation exposes complexity fields in its output, but the script itself still depends on explicit `--level` input rather than invoking `recommend-level.sh` automatically.\n34: \n35: When `create.sh` picks the number itself, it numbers after the highest numbered folder in the root it writes to, and the folders in that root's `z_archive/` count too, so a new packet never takes the number of an archived one. With `--track` it also asks `suggest-subtracks.mjs` for advice and prints at most one `[speckit]` line on stderr: a flat track that clusters gets a pointer to the proposal command, and a packet filed in a sub-track is told when no sub-track fits its description or when another one fits better. The line is advice only. A missing node, a missing tool or a failed run prints nothing and the scaffold continues.\n36: \n37: ### Upgrade & Validation\n38: \n39: Upgrade automation is handled by `upgrade-level.sh`. It detects the current level from explicit markers in `spec.md` or from the presence of `acceptance-criteria.md` and `decision-record.md`, validates that upgrades only move upward, creates timestamped `.backup-*` directories, and rolls back markdown changes if an upgrade step fails. The upgrade path is chained, so a request like Level 1 to Level 3 passes through the intermediate Level 2 transformations automatically. New files are introduced only when needed, such as `acceptance-criteria.md` for Level 2 and `decision-record.md` for Level 3, while higher-level addendum sections are injected or appended into existing documents. `--dry-run`, `--json`, `--verbose`, and `--keep-backups` support inspection and automation use cases.\n40: \n41: Completion measurement and completion gating are separated on purpose. `calculate-completeness.sh` scans the root spec files for placeholder markers such as `[PLACEHOLDER]`, `[TODO: ...]`, and `[NEEDS CLARIFICATION: ...]`, computes per-file and overall completion percentages with `bc`, and can add quality checks for minimum word counts and required sections. `check-completion.sh` then evaluates the verification checklist in `tasks.md`, and the acceptance-criteria table when the document exists, as a release gate: it inherits priority from headings when needed, treats untagged items as blocking, requires evidence markers on completed P0 and P1 tasks, supports `--strict` to make P2 mandatory, and returns one of a fixed set of statuses such as `P0_INCOMPLETE`, `EVIDENCE_MISSING`, `AC_UNMET`, or `COMPLETE`.\n42: \n43: ### Post-Action Behavior\n44: \n45: Archival is handled by `archive.sh`. A folder goes into the `z_archive/` beside it: `specs/z_archive/` for a packet at the specs root, the track's own `specs/<track>/z_archive/` for a packet in a track, a declared sub-track's own `z_archive/` for a packet in that sub-track and the parent's own `z_archive/` for a phase. A restore returns a folder to the home its archive sits in, under its own name when its number is free there. When a live packet under another name already holds that number, the restore does not reuse it: the packet takes the number after the highest one used in that home, live or archived, and `archive.sh` hands the move to `relocate.mjs --from <archived> --to <new> --apply` so every record that names the packet follows the new number. If `relocate.mjs` is missing or its run fails, the restore exits with status 1 and prints the command to rerun. A folder or link already at the exact restore path is refused. The list shows every archive's entries by the path that restores them. Archives are only looked for along packet homes: the specs root, tracks, declared sub-tracks and the numbered packets and phases reached from them. A folder without the sub-track declaration is not a home, so copies of a specs tree kept in a packet's research are never listed or restored from. After an archive and after a plain restore, the script re-derives the moved packet's recorded paths with `repair-derived.cjs`, asks the description cache's own producer to rebuild `specs/descriptions.json` when that file exists and runs `refresh-track-roots.mjs` for the track. A move inside a declared sub-track refreshes the sub-track's list and its track's list together, so each root's `children_ids` stays equal to its packets and the pre-push track-root gate has nothing to block. A tree without the cache file gets none, and a failed refresh is a warning that names the rerun and leaves the move in place. A phase parent's `children_ids` is left as it is: its writer adds children and drop... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n46: \n47: Taken together, the lifecycle tooling currently covers recommendation, creation, decomposition, upgrade, measurement, completion gating, and archival, but it does so through explicit scri","truncated":true}
{"type":"tool_result","callId":"chatcmpl-tool-8629385eb9ceb228","status":"completed","result":"<path>/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Skilled/.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/manual-testing-playbook/tooling-and-scripts/spec-lifecycle-automation.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: title: \"237 -- Spec Lifecycle Automation\"\n3: description: \"This scenario validates spec lifecycle automation for `237`. It focuses on confirming the lifecycle toolchain exposes recommendation, upgrade, completeness, completion, and archival entrypoints.\"\n4: version: 1.6.0.13\n5: id: tooling-and-scripts-spec-lifecycle-automation\n6: expected_workflow_mode: UNKNOWN\n7: expected_leaf_resources: []\n8: ---\n9: \n10: # 237 -- Spec Lifecycle Automation\n11: \n12: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n13: \n14: This scenario validates spec lifecycle automation for `237`. It focuses on confirming the lifecycle toolchain exposes recommendation, upgrade, completeness, completion, and archival entrypoints.\n15: \n16: ---\n17: \n18: ## 2. SCENARIO CONTRACT\n19: \n20: \n21: - Objective: Confirm lifecycle tool availability across recommendation, upgrade, completeness, completion, and archival surfaces.\n22: - Real user request: `Please validate Spec Lifecycle Automation against bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/recommend-level.sh --help and tell me whether the expected signals are present: help output available for lifecycle entrypoints; upgrade regression suite passes; completeness JSON is emitted; completion gate returns a stable status.`\n23: - Prompt: `Validate Spec Lifecycle Automation against bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/recommend-level.sh --help and report cited pass/fail evidence.`\n24: - Expected execution process: Run the documented TEST EXECUTION command sequence, capture the transcript and evidence, compare the observed output against the expected signals, and return the pass/fail verdict.\n25: - Expected signals: help output available for lifecycle entrypoints; upgrade regression suite passes; completeness JSON is emitted; completion gate returns a stable status\n26: - Desired user-visible outcome: A concise pass/fail verdict with the main reason and cited evidence.\n27: - Pass/fail: PASS if the lifecycle scripts collectively expose the documented workflow and the known-good fixture behaves consistently\n28: \n29: ---\n30: \n31: ## 3. TEST EXECUTION\n32: \n33: ### Prompt\n34: \n35: ```\n36: Validate Spec Lifecycle Automation against bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/recommend-level.sh --help and report cited pass/fail evidence.\n37: ```\n38: \n39: ### Commands\n40: \n41: 1. `bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/recommend-level.sh --help`\n42: 2. `bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/tests/test-upgrade-level.sh`\n43: 3. `bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/calculate-completeness.sh .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/test-fixtures/063-template-compliant-level3 --json`\n44: 4. `bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/check-completion.sh .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/test-fixtures/063-template-compliant-level3 --json`\n45: 5. `bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/archive.sh --help`\n46: \n47: ### Expected\n48: \n49: Recommendation and archive help text is available; upgrade-level regression suite passes; completeness JSON emits percentage-style data; completion status is returned for the fixture\n50: \n51: ### Evidence\n52: \n53: Command 1: `bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/recommend-level.sh --help`\n54: \n55: ```text\n56: SpecKit Level Recommendation Algorithm\n57: \n58: Analyzes task complexity factors and recommends the appropriate SpecKit level.\n59: \n60: USAGE:\n61:   recommend-level.sh [OPTIONS]\n62: \n63: OPTIONS:\n64:   --loc <number>       Lines of code estimate (required)\n65:   --files <number>     Number of files to modify (required)\n66:   --auth               Task involves authentication/authorization changes\n67:   --api                Task involves API changes\n68:   --db                 Task involves database changes\n69:   --architectural      Task involves architectural changes\n70:   --recommend-phases   Include phase scoring in output (default)\n71:   --no-recommend-phases Disable phase scoring output\n72:   --phase-threshold <N> Override phase score threshold (default 25)\n73:   --json, -j           Output in JSON format\n74:   --help, -h           Show this help message\n75: \n76: EXAMPLES:\n77:   # Simple task\n78:   recommend-level.sh --loc 50 --files 2\n79: \n80:   # Medium complexity with API\n81:   recommend-level.sh --loc 200 --files 5 --api\n82: \n83:   # Complex full-stack feature\n84:   recommend-level.sh --loc 500 --files 15 --auth --api --db --architectural\n85: \n86:   # With phase recommendation (default behavior)\n87:   recommend-level.sh --loc 1000 --files 20 --architectural --api --db --recommend-phases\n88: \n89:   # Disable phase recommendation fields\n90:   recommend-level.sh --loc 1000 --files 20 --architectural --no-recommend-phases\n91: \n92:   # JSON output for scripting\n93:   recommend-level.sh --loc 100 --files 3 --json\n94: \n95: LEVELS:\n96:   Level 0 (Quick):        <25 points  - Trivial changes, no formal spec needed\n97:   Level 1 (Baseline):     25-44       - Standard tasks, basic documentation\n98:   Level 2 (Verification): 45-69       - Complex tasks, full verification needed\n99:   Level 3 (Full):         70+         - Critical/architectural, comprehensive docs\n100: ```\n101: \n102: Command 2: `bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/tests/test-upgrade-level.sh`\n103: \n104: ```text\n105: \n106: ────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n107:   upgrade-level.sh Test Suite\n108: ────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n109: \n110: ── Shell-Common Guard ──\n111:   PASS: Missing helper exits with exact code 1 and clear message\n112: \n113: ── Invalid Input ──\n114:   PASS: No args produces usage error (exit=1)\n115:   PASS: Missing --to flag produces error (exit=1)\n116:   PASS: Invalid --to value rejected (exit=1)\n117: \n118: ── Missing spec.md ──\n119:   PASS: Empty dir (no spec.md) produces graceful error (exit=1)\n120:   PASS: Non-existent directory produces error (exit=1)\n121: \n122: ── Level Detection ──\n123:   PASS: Detects Level 2 from table format '| **Level** | 2 |' (exit=1)\n124:   PASS: Detects Level 3 from SPECKIT_LEVEL marker (exit=1)\n125: \n126: ── Already At Target ──\n127:   PASS: Already at target level = no-op with error (exit=1)\n128: \n129: ── Dry-Run Mode ──\n130:   PASS: Dry-run exits 0 (exit=0)\n131:   PASS: Dry-run did not create checklist.md\n132:   PASS: Dry-run left existing files unchanged\n133:   PASS: Dry-run did not create backup directory\n134:   PASS: Dry-run output mentions DRY RUN\n135: \n136: ────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n137: ────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n138: \n139:   Results: 14 passed, 0 failed, 0 skipped (of 14)\n140: ```\n141: \n142: Command 3: `bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/calculate-completeness.sh .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/test-fixtures/063-template-compliant-level3 --json`\n143: \n144: ```json\n145: {\n146:   \"spec_folder\": \".skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/test-fixtures/063-template-compliant-level3\",\n147:   \"files_analyzed\": 4,\n148:   \"overall_completion\": 100,\n149:   \"total_placeholders\": 0,\n150:   \"total_lines\": 527,\n151:   \"files\": {},\n152:   \"quality\": {\n153:     \"enabled\": false\n154:   }\n155: }\n156: ```\n157: \n158: Command 4: `bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/check-completion.sh .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/test-fixtures/063-template-compliant-level3 --json`\n159: \n160: ```json\n161: {\n162:   \"folder\": \".skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/test-fixtures/063-template-compliant-l","truncated":true}
