{"type":"tool_result","callId":"chatcmpl-tool-83270e337b385b01","status":"completed","result":".skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/retrieval/retrieval-conventions.md:36:Ripgrep is an evidence producer, never the relevance ranker. It returns matches, paths and lines. The caller decides what any of it is worth.\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/retrieval/retrieval-conventions.md:58:| `memory_quick_search` | The path-only recipe, Section 2.2 |\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/retrieval/retrieval-conventions.md:83:- `--glob '!**/z_archive/**'`, `--glob '!**/node_modules/**'` and `--glob '!**/.git/**'` keep archived packets, vendored trees and repository internals out of the result set; the last one exists because `--hidden` would otherwise let ripgrep into `.git`.\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/retrieval/retrieval-conventions.md:84:- `--include-archive` is the wrapper's opt-in archive mode, and the default is unchanged: without the flag every recipe keeps `--glob '!**/z_archive/**'`. With it `rg-wrapper.mjs` drops that one glob and keeps the other three exclusions. Every printed hit under a `z_archive` segment ends with `[archived]`, and the JSON rows of the structured and count recipes carry `archived: true|false`. A second invocation, the JSON lane, lists the `description.json` and `graph-metadata.json` files of archived packets that hold the phrase under a `json    :` section, reported as `jsonLane` in the JSON record. It is its own invocation because of Section 2.5. The exit is `0` when either lane matches.\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/retrieval/retrieval-conventions.md:103:The path-only and count recipes take the same `--limit`. The wrapper sorts their lists before the cut, paths by name and counts by busiest file first, because ripgrep's own order changes between runs. Both report `fileCount`, `limit`, `truncated` and `hidden`, and the human form ends with the same `+N more files` line. The count recipe also reports `matchCount` and the hidden match total. The path-only recipe stops at one match per file, so its `hidden.matches` is `null`.\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/retrieval/retrieval-conventions.md:148:`--json` cannot be combined with the path-only mode (`-l` or `--files-with-matches`), the count mode (`-c` or `--count`) or `--count-matches`. Each output mode is its own recipe, and the wrapper parses one shape at a time.\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/retrieval/retrieval-conventions.md:189:**Keyed-lane zero-hit fallback.** `lookup-trigger-index.mjs` adds candidates from spec documents the committed index does not list yet when the index returns no scored candidate. With the fallback on (the default), exit `1` means neither the index nor the fallback found a scored candidate, and a fallback hit exits `0` like any other match. It runs only on a zero-hit and only inside a git work tree, and it reads each unindexed document's declared `trigger_phrases`, never its body text. `--no-stale-fallback` turns it off, and so does an explicit `--index`, which restores index-only exit semantics. It walks the tree the index read belongs to: the host's own tree when a linked project reads its host's index, and no tree at all when the index lives outside the caller's repository, as the packaged index of a linked install does, because that index's paths name other documents.\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/retrieval/retrieval-conventions.md:198:| Path-only, Section 2.2 | `0` | 15 paths |\n.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/retrieval/retrieval-conventions.md:213:Ripgrep supplies matches, paths and lines. It never ranks relevance, and no document, plan or wrapper comment may imply that it does. Ordering is the caller's job, applied after parsing, using this stable tuple.\n.skilled/commands/speckit/search.md:3:argument-hint: \"<query> [--packet <specFolder>] [--triggers] [--paths] [--count] [--include-archive]\"\n.skilled/commands/speckit/search.md:98:| Which files mention it (`--paths`) | Section 2.2 | `--files-with-matches` |\n.skilled/commands/speckit/search.md:116:A phrase that only an archived packet holds exits `1` without the flag and `0` with it, and each archived path ends `[archived]`. The flag drops the `!**/z_archive/**` glob from the recipe and marks every printed hit under a `z_archive` segment with `[archived]`. The JSON rows of the structured and count recipes carry `archived: true|false`. A second invocation then lists the `description.json` and `graph-metadata.json` files of archived packets that hold the phrase, under a `json    :` section, and the exit is `0` when either lane matches. The mode is opt-in only. `/speckit:resume` excludes archives and has no such flag, and the trigger-index lane never reads them.\n.skilled/commands/speckit/search.md:119:2. Run exactly one recipe. Never combine `--json` with the path-only or count mode: ripgrep does not reject the combination, the last output-mode flag wins silently, and a JSONL parser handed count output sees an empty result rather than an error.\n.skilled/commands/speckit/search.md:121:4. Keep the wrapper's `results` in the order it returned them: it already applied the ranking tuple in `retrieval-conventions.md` Section 5 — evidence field, then normalized match class, then relative path and one-based line. Order raw-recipe output with the same tuple. Ripgrep supplies matches, paths and lines; it never ranks relevance, and no rendered output may imply that it does.\n"}
