{"type":"thinking","text":"The sub-track proposal playbook does not cover the phase 009 refusal (non-NNN-slug) or existing-folder name collision. The expected \"five-entry map\" is still valid. The playbook's pass/fail doesn't mention verifying preflight acceptance (phase 009's guarantee). The manual testing scenario should ideally include a case; but a playbook isn't necessarily updated for every edge case. The review focus says playbook never updated for 006-010 behavior. Candidate finding: playbook 493 omits the non-NNN-slug refused proposal and the map-accepted-by-relocate check; playbook 494 omits archivedPaths in path JSON; playbook 492 omits exit 3/resume/case-fold. These are P2 doc gaps. Should I report each as separate findings? That may be too many. I can consolidate by behavior and cite the primary doc, with affectedSurfaceHints listing the other docs.\n\nBut the iteration contract requires findingDetails with id/severity/title. Fine.\n\nI need to decide the scope of findings so that the verdict is defensible. The docs stale issues are mostly P1/P2. Are any P0? P0 = blocker (exploitable security, destructive data loss). A stale doc that tells operators to manually fix could cause mistakes, but not destructive. No P0 likely. So verdict will be CONDITIONAL (if any P1) or PASS with advisories (if only P2). The operator focus strongly implies they expect material findings, but severity must be evidence-based. Let me use P1 for:\n- SKILL.md:295 \"Next spec number\" command ignores z_archive, contradicting D4 and phase 003/008 behavior → following it can allocate an archived number (the very failure the feature exists to prevent). That's a spec mismatch with real operational impact. P1.\n- feature-catalog spec-packet-relocation.md:66 enumerates exit codes without 3 and says 2 means all failures; phase 008 added 3. Operators misdiagnose. Is this P1? It's an incomplete enumeration in a \"current-state\" catalog. I'd call P1 (spec mismatch) because the catalog is the authoritative feature reference and the exit code is a public contract.\n- feature-catalog spec-lifecycle-automation.md:45 says a failed relocate run makes restore exit 1; with exit 3 the restore now exits 0 with warning. That's a direct contradiction of current behavior for a documented path. P1.\n- changelog:67 missing exit 3 + \"Exit 2 means the plan was refused or a step failed\" (still true for step failure, but not for validation-only failure after all steps). Incomplete. Could be P2 (release notes snapshot) or P1. The changelog is user-facing release documentation; the exit code is a contract. I'd group with the feature catalog exit-code finding or separate P2. To avoid double counting, I'll report the feature catalog as P1 and the changelog as P2 with same behavior (documents stale in two places).\n- feature catalog rg-archive-search.md:28 \"paths stays plain strings\" omits archivedPaths, and the path JSON is supposed to mark archived paths (completion criterion). Since the catalog explicitly describes the JSON shape and omits the new field, that's a spec mismatch. P1 or P2. The parent criterion says path record marks same paths; the doc says only rows carry archived. I'd call P1? It's a documentation gap about a new contract. Hmm.\n\nLet me think about how many P1s are appropriate. The task is a documentation alignment review, and the operator says docs were never updated for ten behaviors. The natural outcome: multiple P1 findings for docs that contradict current behavior, P2 for omissions. But if every doc omission is P1, the verdict is CONDITIONAL with many. The severity contract says P1 = spec mismatch/must-fix. Since the review target is the spec folder, and the docs are the product, stale current-state docs are spec mismatches. The operator likely expects a CONDITIONAL verdict and a remediation plan to update docs. That seems right.\n\nHowever, don't inflate. Let me carefully verify each claimed doc line before writing. And check if some docs already got updated (e.g., spec/README.md is updated; feature catalog spec-packet-relocation may have been partially updated with phase 006/007? It mentions \"a run that stopped on a failed step is finished by running the same map again\" (phase 006), but not exit 3, case-fold, bare names, pre-archive. It seems phase 006-010 updates didn't touch it. The changelog is v2.13.0.0 (phases 001-005) and wasn't updated. The feature catalog spec-lifecycle-automation line 35 mentions archive-aware numbering for create.sh (phase 003) but not phase 008 restore verdict/mixed track. The runtime/cli/spec/README.md line 120 is updated through phase 008/009 (exit 3, rerun, preflight name). So some docs did get updated. Good.\n\nLet me check the manual playbook spec-validation-archive-reach and feature catalog spec-validation-rule-engine for archivedPaths/exit 3? The validation playbook is about --include-archive. The review focus lists phase 006-010 docs never updated. Maybe the playbook for validation has the old reach statement. Let me read it."}
