erences/structure/phase-definitions.md`: phase numbering gap? Let me decide. It says \"Add the next sequential child phase\" — I'd flag as P2 for archive-aware phase numbering. It's a small but real omission. The operator explicitly lists it. Yes.\n`references/workflows/quick-reference.md`: line 135 \"numbers the packet from the highest number in use\" is actually correct (now includes archives); line 265 \"next sequential child phase\" omits archived. Could cite both phase-definitions:255 and quick-reference:265 in one finding? The finding file must be one path:line. I'll cite phase-definitions.md:255 as primary and list quick-reference:265 in affectedSurfaceHints.\n\nNow \"late-step ledger marks and resume\": docs that describe relocate behavior:\n- feature-catalog spec-packet-relocation.md:68 says a stopped run is finished by rerunning the same map. That's actually aligned with phase 006. It doesn't mention late-step ledger marks, but the operator-visible behavior is covered. So no finding for resume? The focus says \"late-step ledger marks and resume ... never updated for\". But the catalog line 68 does cover resume (maybe it was already there from phase 001). The ledger marks are internal. The docs don't need internal details. So maybe no finding for resume; note in Confirmed-Clean that the catalog already states rerun behavior, and the changelog's --renumber caveat remains accurate. Good — that's a \"docs need no change\" item. But the review focus specifically says \"late-step ledger marks and resume\" as a stale doc gap. Hmm. The external behavior is documented at feature catalog line 68; the ledger marks are implementation detail. If the operator expects docs to explain the ledger, the `runtime/cli/spec/README.md:120` says \"A rerun after status 3 finds nothing to do; a second --apply of an applied map also finds nothing to do, and rerunning a map that stopped part-way finishes it.\" That covers it. So I can say the behavior is already documented in the spec README and catalog; no gap there. But perhaps the changelog's line 67 says \"A map that stopped part-way finishes on the next run\" — yes. So resume docs are clean. I'll rule that out.\n\n\"case-folded duplicate refusal\": no doc mentions; feature catalog refusal list says \"named by two entries\" (could be interpreted to include case spellings, but not explicitly). It's an edge case. P2.\n\n\"reworded archive signal\": out of manifest (graph-metadata.json excluded). Note in scope/edge case. No finding.\n\nNow, what about the *presentation assets* and sk-create-command alignment? The presentation assets in manifest are for create/deep/speckit. Phase 002 changed option C wording in them; they are aligned. Phase 009 reworded the archive signal maybe in `graph-metadata.json` only. The search presentation lacks archivedPaths; that's a command-doc gap (P2). The `.skilled/commands/speckit/README.txt:105` also omits archivedPaths. Could be included in the F4 finding as affected surfaces (same behavior, multiple docs). The operator asked exact file:line for each gap; F4 can cite one primary and list others in evidence. I'll add a separate finding for search.md + presentation if needed. Let me keep it to one finding per behavior with multiple citations in evidence/affectedSurfaceHints, but the `file` field has one primary. Hmm, \"Cite exact file:line for each gap\" — I'll cite multiple within the description, e.g., \"search.md:116, README.txt:105, search-presentation.txt:120\". The finding should include all evidence refs. The reducer stores one file:line, but the narrative can hold multiple. That's acceptable.\n\nGiven the 5 iterations, I'll structure:\n- Iter 1: correctness dimension — verify code behavior and map to docs; find F1 (SKILL next-number), F2 (exit 3), F3 (restore verdict/mixed track). Operator focus mapping.\n- Iter 2: traceability — version/contract alignment of SKILL.md, references, changelog; F4 (archivedPaths docs), F5 (folder-structure three cases), F6 (phase numbering archive-aware), F7 (changelog exit 3/archivedPaths).\n- Iter 3: maintainability — feature catalog & playbook against sk-doc standards; F8 (feature catalog rewrite coverage), F9 (case-fold refusal), F10 (playbook relocation missing exit3/resume/case), F11 (playbook sub-track preflight), F12 (playbook archive path JSON).\n- Iter 4: security — check path handling/archive search/restore refusal docs; maybe F13 (security-relevant: root README/command docs don't state symlink refusals? Not likely). Could be a clean pass with P2 findings about archive path handling docs. Or use iteration 4 to consolidate documentation gaps in command docs/system README (maintainability). Then iteration 5 cross-reference + verdict.\n\nWait, the four dimensions required: correctness, security, traceability, maintainability. I need at least one full iteration per dimension. I'll assign:\n- Iteration 1: correctness (code behavior vs docs) + focus.\n- Iteration 2: security (archive search path handling, restore/refusal, case-fold; docs coverage of security boundaries) + focus.\n- Iteration 3: traceability (SKILL.md, references, changelog, standards alignment) + focus.\n- Iteration 4: maintainability (feature catalog, playbooks, READMEs, command docs) + focus.\n- Iteration 5: cross-dimension consolidation / unresolved docs, convergence.\n\nSecurity findings: Are there security docs stale? The archive search path handling docs: `rg-wrapper` has `--include-archive`, symlink behavior? The docs say symlinked archive not followed? For rg, it follows symlinks? Let me check if rg-wrapper excludes symlinks or if z_archive symlinks are searched. The archive tools refuse symlinked z_archive. Search might follow symlinks, potentially reading outside specs. Hmm. That could be a security issue in code, not docs. Let me investigate briefly: rg by default doesn't follow symlinks (no -L), so fine. `path record` doesn't expose sensitive data. The restore refusals (path traversal, symlink) are do