{"type":"thinking","text":"Good. Now iteration 2: security dimension. I need to review the security-relevant surfaces in the manifest:\n- archive search path handling: rg-wrapper.mjs flags, `--search-root`, `--root`, hidden, symlinks, `--include-archive` scope. Is there a security boundary? Archive search returns archived packets; untrusted content? The docs say archives are included only on request. No secrets.\n- restore/refusal: archive.sh path traversal, symlinked archives, mixed-track refusal. These are path-safety guards. Docs coverage: archive.sh help lines 84-89, runtime/cli/spec/README.md:114, feature catalog line 45 (stale).\n- case-folded duplicate refusal in relocate prevents two entries resolving to the same folder on case-insensitive volumes. This is a safety guard. Docs: feature catalog refusal list omits; runtime README line 120 says \"a name that is not NNN-slug\" and \"destination collision\" but maybe not case fold. Let me check README line 120 wording: \"Preflight refuses a destination collision, a taken number (archived siblings count), a track left mixed, ...\". \"destination collision\" might cover case-fold duplicates. Hmm.\n- suggest-subtracks path handling? It reads metadata only, no writes; safe.\n- create.sh `--path` traversal documented.\n\nSecurity finding possibilities:\n1. `rg-wrapper.mjs` `--include-archive` may follow symlinked `z_archive/` and search outside specs. Does rg follow symlinks? By default no (-L off). The code's `--hidden` doesn't affect symlinks. So safe.\n2. The path JSON `archivedPaths` and `jsonLane` may leak paths? No.\n3. `archive.sh` mixed-track refusal uses `relocate.mjs` dry run; if `relocate.mjs` is missing, it returns 0 and proceeds with the move, potentially leaving a mixed track. Wait — line 371: `[[ -f \"$relocate_script\" ]] || return 0` — if relocate is missing, the refusal is skipped. Then the restore proceeds, and the next sweep fails. That's a robustness gap, not security. But the docs say a mixed-track restore is refused; if relocate is missing, it isn't. The phase 008 goal says restore refuses mixed track. Is this a P1 code finding? The reach: a repo with archive.sh but no relocate.mjs (e.g., a trimmed install) could restore into a mixed track. But the manifest includes both; in a shipped skill they coexist. If relocate is missing, many other features fail. I'd note it as P2 or ruled out. The review-core reach calibration: the code's stated contract is \"restore refuses mixed track\"; if relocate.mjs missing, the guard silently skips. Is that input in reach? A missing tool is an installation state; the tool prints a warning? No. The code returns 0 silently. This could be a P2 hardening finding. But the review focus is docs; maybe not.\n\n4. `archive.sh` restore next-number path: `to_id` built from `name:4` and `next`, fine. The mixed-track refusal is read before the number collision check; good.\n\n5. `relocate.mjs` case-fold duplicate: uses `device`/`inode` of parents; on a case-folding volume, two paths with different case that don't exist yet collapse. Need to check the code for correctness; it looks deliberate.\n\n6. `rg-wrapper` path record `archivedPaths` includes only paths matching `isArchivedPath` — any path with a `z_archive` segment, including a live packet named `z_archive`? Fine.\n\nSecurity docs gap: The archive.sh help states refusal for symlinked archive and dot segments; the feature catalog lifecycle paragraph mentions path safety? It says \"A folder or link already at the exact restore path is refused\", but not symlink/dot segments. The CLI README line 114 mentions \"An archive or restore path with a . or .. segment is refused\" etc. The docs are decent.\n\nMaybe the security iteration will find:\n- F004 (P2, security/traceability): The feature-catalog restore paragraph omits the mixed-track refusal, which is the one guard that stops a restore from creating a mixed track that later breaks the sweep. That's already F003; don't double count.\n- F004 (P2, security): `spec-packet-relocation.md:55-64` refusal list names \"a tracked source with uncommitted changes\" etc. but omits the case-folded duplicate refusal, which is the guard that stops two spellings of one folder from moving the same files twice on a case-insensitive filesystem. This is the case-fold behavior. P2 security/hardening. I planned F9 (P2) for this in maintainability; better to put it in security iteration as a P2, since it's a safety guard.\n- F005 (P2, security): The documented `archive.sh --restore`/`--list` symlink guarantees are in the CLI README and help but the feature catalog paragraph line 45 doesn't mention them; however the catalog's job isn't to enumerate every guard. Could be noise. Skip.\n- Maybe a P1: The SKILL.md quick reference \"Next spec number\" is already F001.\n- Could find a real doc gap: `archive.sh --help` (playbook copy) line 84-89 says \"A restore whose number a live packet already holds lands on the next free number\" but doesn't say the mixed-track refusal. The help text is the operator's primary contract. The phase 008 behavior is not documented there. That's a P1/P2 security-adjacent doc gap. But F003 covers the catalog; I could add a separate finding for the archive.sh help text itself (runtime/cli/spec/README.md:114 also). Hmm.\n\nMaybe the security iteration can be a clean pass with one P2 finding and a lot of \"confirmed-clean\" for path guards. That's acceptable. But the review should be substantive; iteration 2 can also examine the phase 009 suggest-subtracks refusal and the archive search default exclusion from a security/least-privilege perspective. The docs:\n- `rg-archive-search.md:26` says a path counts as archived when a segment is exactly `z_archive`. Good.\n- `search.md:110-116` says opt-in. Good.\n- `retrieval-conventions.md:83-84` says default exclusion. Good.\n- `search-presentation.txt:120-128` says render rate. Good (except archivedPaths).\nNo security gaps. Clean.\n\nCase-folded duplicate refusal: let me read relocate.mjs 460-520 and 520-570 to understand the guard and its doc coverage."}
